1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,160
It's a terrible storm, and of course, if you've been listening to this series,

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:06,820
which we hope you have, you will know that it strikes at the worst possible moment,

3
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:11,580
when France is gripped by this political and financial crisis.

4
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:16,320
And the storm is then followed by a drought across France,

5
00:00:16,540 --> 00:00:18,120
then suddenly a very hot summer,

6
00:00:18,220 --> 00:00:23,140
and farmers are writing to the court in Versailles sending letters asking for help.

7
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:27,780
A once ravishing countryside has been reduced to an arid desert.

8
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:31,240
And there's a real sense of kind of, of catastrophe.

9
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:32,020
Is that an Arab?

10
00:00:32,260 --> 00:00:33,020
That was Scottish again.

11
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:38,020
There's a real sense of incipient catastrophe,

12
00:00:38,340 --> 00:00:41,860
which of course, in the context of everything we were talking about in the last episode,

13
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:43,480
means that...

14
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:44,300
It's not helpful, is it?

15
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:45,520
It's not helpful at all.

16
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:51,260
The interesting thing, actually, is that last time we were talking about the Ancien Régime,

17
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:55,740
and the sort of, how the stereotype of it is so misleading.

18
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:57,400
And so it is with this case.

19
00:00:57,780 --> 00:01:01,420
You would think, if you had read A Tale of Two Cities and all of the sort of,

20
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:05,460
you know, you used the caricatures of the French Revolution and the French elite,

21
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:07,800
that nobody did anything to help.

22
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:08,940
And you would be wrong.

23
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:12,860
Because actually, the Archbishop of Paris launches this big charity drive.

24
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:18,660
The king gives a million livres, even though, you know, the monarchy has pretty much run out of money.

25
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:24,280
There's a national lottery is organized and it raises 12 million livres to pay for relief.

26
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:26,680
But as we will see,

27
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:27,760
it's,

28
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:31,540
it is not remotely enough to rescue the situation, because, Tom,

29
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:35,940
nature has more storms in store.

30
00:01:36,660 --> 00:01:37,020
Wow.

31
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:39,600
So the elements are combining against the monarchy.

32
00:01:39,740 --> 00:01:40,240
They are indeed.

33
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:41,560
And the Ancien Régime.

34
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:42,640
Is in a mess.

35
00:01:43,020 --> 00:01:44,040
I think it's fair to say.

36
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:46,440
Were you trying to think of a meteorological metaphor for the...

37
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:47,980
No, I've moved on from the meteorological metaphors.

38
00:01:47,980 --> 00:01:49,480
I was maybe thinking of a building one.

39
00:01:49,540 --> 00:01:51,720
I was thinking perhaps it's tottering on its foundations.

40
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:53,060
It is tottering on its foundations.

41
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:56,180
Because as we discussed at the end of the last episode,

42
00:01:57,780 --> 00:02:02,820
some, something looking very much like a French Revolution has already broken out in Grenoble

43
00:02:02,820 --> 00:02:07,840
and in Dauphine in the southeast of France and has spread to other provinces.

44
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:13,460
Louis XVI, in desperation, has announced that he will call the Estates General.

45
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:22,480
And we ended last time with the bombshell that Archbishop Brienne, the finance minister,

46
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:27,140
has basically declared bankruptcy, at least partial bankruptcy,

47
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:28,640
And it's ironic, isn't it?

48
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:31,320
Because he doesn't believe in God, but all these acts of God have now come to France.

49
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:31,840
Yes, ironically.

50
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:33,120
Well, he didn't believe in the deficit.

51
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:35,820
And then he was appointed to look at the books.

52
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:37,660
He's on a learning curve.

53
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:37,780
Yeah, he is.

54
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:45,340
Well, his learning curve is abruptly terminated a week later on the 25th of August when he is booted out.

55
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:50,520
And the monarchy turned to the one person who everybody in France thinks can rescue the situation.

56
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:52,720
This is a man we mentioned very briefly last time.

57
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:55,180
His name is Jean-Jacques Necker.

58
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:57,460
And he is not...

59
00:02:57,780 --> 00:02:58,240
A courtier.

60
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:00,220
He is not an aristocrat.

61
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:01,760
He is not even French.

62
00:03:02,040 --> 00:03:05,840
He is a Swiss Protestant from a Genevan banking family.

63
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:11,300
And he is meant to be the great financial whiz kid of Western Europe.

64
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:16,140
Because the Swiss even then have a kind of reputation for sobriety

65
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:19,840
and the kind of man that you would trust to sort out your bank balance.

66
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:20,520
Totally, they do.

67
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:21,220
Yes, exactly.

68
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:23,260
Necker is a...

69
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:27,140
It seems odd now from our perspective, all these centuries later,

70
00:03:27,780 --> 00:03:32,940
to comprehend how a Swiss banker could be such a folk hero across France.

71
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:33,300
Yeah, I know.

72
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:37,960
But absolutely, there was a sense in France in the late 18th century

73
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:41,560
that Protestants, although they are weird, alien and heretical,

74
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:45,260
nevertheless, they're very boring and they can be trusted.

75
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:47,280
And Swiss Protestants...

76
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:49,400
Yeah, they're kind of black sober suits.

77
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:50,140
Exactly.

78
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:51,360
All that kind of thing.

79
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:53,160
So when I was reading up on Necker,

80
00:03:53,320 --> 00:03:57,200
I was reminded of how a lot of people in Britain thought about the Canadian bank,

81
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:01,640
the Canadian national banker, Mark Carney, who...

82
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:02,980
A listener to The Rest Is History.

83
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:04,320
A listener to The Rest Is History, exactly.

84
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:06,820
Who ran the Bank of England.

85
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:08,960
And there was a perception, I think, in Britain,

86
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:11,700
that he was very measured, very Canadian,

87
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:14,680
very kind of controlled and kind of apparently very sober.

88
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:16,720
I mean, I'm not saying he wasn't sober, but...

89
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:18,280
I'm just saying that's how...

90
00:04:18,280 --> 00:04:19,240
The avoidance of doubt.

91
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:20,820
That's how he appeared.

92
00:04:21,280 --> 00:04:21,900
He's listening.

93
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:23,740
Dominic is not accusing you of being drunk.

94
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:24,300
No, I'm not.

95
00:04:24,980 --> 00:04:25,880
But I'm saying...

96
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:27,180
I'm saying...

97
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:28,780
People who knew nothing about Mark Carney,

98
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:32,160
precisely because he was an outsider, not tainted by British politics,

99
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:35,820
people attributed him with sort of extraordinary powers

100
00:04:35,820 --> 00:04:39,580
and they saw him as a figure who represented stability and solidity

101
00:04:39,580 --> 00:04:40,960
and he wasn't...

102
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:42,200
And also neutrality.

103
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:43,220
Yeah, neutrality, exactly.

104
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:45,340
And objectivity, not having too many vested interests.

105
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:49,620
And I guess that's why people think, you know, Necker is a deus ex machina.

106
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:50,100
They do, indeed.

107
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:53,320
Someone who can be introduced to kind of cut through

108
00:04:53,320 --> 00:04:55,980
all the Gordian knots that have been...

109
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:56,040
Yeah.

110
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:58,040
Strangling the French economy.

111
00:04:58,240 --> 00:04:58,880
We love it.

112
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:01,480
I mean, not just one Gordian knot, according to you,

113
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:02,540
but multiple Gordian knots.

114
00:05:03,660 --> 00:05:04,400
All of them.

115
00:05:04,580 --> 00:05:05,440
So, yeah, Necker...

116
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:06,800
Sprouting, like Hydra's heads.

117
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:09,680
And Necker, to be fair, is brilliant at...

118
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:13,700
I mean, the one thing he had done is he'd got loans for the American war,

119
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:16,020
you might argue, with disastrous consequences.

120
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:17,720
He's very good at getting loans

121
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:19,660
because he has so many friends in the banking business.

122
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:22,740
So as soon as he arrives, he says,

123
00:05:22,780 --> 00:05:24,060
I can do what Brienne could not.

124
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:25,880
I can get my friends to...

125
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:28,240
I can lend you money at decent rates.

126
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:31,500
I will be able to tide us over for at least another year

127
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:33,960
until the Estates General can meet

128
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:35,860
and we can actually sort this thing out

129
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:38,840
and perhaps set up some new constitutional body

130
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:41,960
or whatever it might be that will approve taxes.

131
00:05:42,380 --> 00:05:43,860
And so the stock exchange,

132
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:46,440
which had been very jittery in the summer of 1788,

133
00:05:46,940 --> 00:05:48,280
kind of returns to normal.

134
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:50,680
And there's a sense, you know,

135
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:52,520
ah, maybe we've got a bit of direction.

136
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:54,160
Maybe we've got a bit of calm.

137
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:55,920
But I think that sense, Tom,

138
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:57,640
would be illusory.

139
00:05:57,840 --> 00:05:59,320
Well, because surely people know

140
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:00,900
that there's an absolute catastrophe looming

141
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:02,600
because the hailstones have wiped out...

142
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:02,960
They do indeed.

143
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:03,620
...the wheat harvest.

144
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:04,020
They do indeed.

145
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:04,720
And that, of course,

146
00:06:04,720 --> 00:06:07,620
has terrible implications for bread.

147
00:06:07,980 --> 00:06:09,840
There were saves of celebrations in Paris

148
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:11,860
that turned into rioting.

149
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:14,880
There was fighting between the Paris Watch

150
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:17,540
and these celebrators stroke rioters.

151
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:21,160
Perhaps 20 people were killed in the fighting.

152
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:23,080
I mean, this is fighting with kind of...

153
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:25,200
This is your classic kind of...

154
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:25,660
Yeah.

155
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:25,920
...the watch.

156
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:27,200
Have sabres and bayonets.

157
00:06:27,280 --> 00:06:28,780
By the end, they're firing into the crowds.

158
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:32,760
Soldiers restore order in the capital eventually.

159
00:06:33,380 --> 00:06:34,700
But exactly as you say,

160
00:06:34,820 --> 00:06:35,780
because of the storms,

161
00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:39,400
everybody knows that something very bad is going to happen.

162
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:42,360
And the reason is this thing about bread.

163
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:45,340
You know, it's the classic thing in the French Revolution

164
00:06:45,340 --> 00:06:48,800
is that you get great hordes of people chanting for bread.

165
00:06:49,040 --> 00:06:50,540
That's one of the kind of images.

166
00:06:51,400 --> 00:06:53,380
And the reason for this is that

167
00:06:53,380 --> 00:06:55,020
three out of four people in France,

168
00:06:55,800 --> 00:06:59,060
subsist almost entirely on basically a particular kind of loaf,

169
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:00,300
which is called the four-pound loaf.

170
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:01,540
If you've got a family of four,

171
00:07:01,640 --> 00:07:05,340
you need two of these loaves every day to feed them.

172
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:07,880
Now, one loaf costs eight sous.

173
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:12,060
And a manual labourer makes about 25 sous a day.

174
00:07:12,260 --> 00:07:13,820
So in other words, he will be spending

175
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:18,540
half, maybe more than half of his daily income

176
00:07:18,540 --> 00:07:20,660
on these two loaves of bread.

177
00:07:20,980 --> 00:07:22,840
And if the bread prices rise,

178
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:25,000
he's in a real...

179
00:07:25,020 --> 00:07:25,300
He's in a real mess.

180
00:07:27,020 --> 00:07:28,300
So by October,

181
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:30,900
the price has already gone from eight to 12 sous.

182
00:07:31,300 --> 00:07:32,660
And eventually, as we will see,

183
00:07:33,260 --> 00:07:35,100
it will peak at about 15 sous

184
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:36,900
around about the time of the...

185
00:07:36,900 --> 00:07:37,820
And it peaks, doesn't it,

186
00:07:37,860 --> 00:07:39,980
on the 14th of July, 1789,

187
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:41,440
the day of the fall of the Bastille.

188
00:07:41,660 --> 00:07:42,840
Now, you could say,

189
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:44,440
we could say,

190
00:07:44,580 --> 00:07:46,280
I mean, there's nothing anyone can do about this.

191
00:07:46,340 --> 00:07:47,620
This is a complete act of God.

192
00:07:47,940 --> 00:07:50,440
You know, the storms have destroyed the harvest.

193
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:51,640
There is not enough...

194
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:54,320
There are not enough crops,

195
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:55,000
there's not enough wheat,

196
00:07:55,020 --> 00:07:56,460
there's not going to be enough flour,

197
00:07:56,600 --> 00:07:57,500
there's not going to be enough bread.

198
00:07:57,620 --> 00:07:58,480
Prices will rise.

199
00:07:58,640 --> 00:08:01,360
But this is an age of pamphlets and of rumours

200
00:08:01,360 --> 00:08:02,760
and therefore of conspiracy theories.

201
00:08:02,760 --> 00:08:03,060
Exactly.

202
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:05,040
And so if something bad happens,

203
00:08:05,240 --> 00:08:06,580
someone must be to blame.

204
00:08:06,700 --> 00:08:07,140
Exactly.

205
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:08,040
That's exactly it.

206
00:08:08,140 --> 00:08:08,800
They have been,

207
00:08:08,920 --> 00:08:12,240
ever since the whole issue was opened by Calonne

208
00:08:12,240 --> 00:08:12,980
and obviously,

209
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:15,280
in many ways, going back even further,

210
00:08:16,300 --> 00:08:18,740
there has been this rhetoric of the king's ministers,

211
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:20,800
the king's ministers who are despotic

212
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:21,540
and they don't care

213
00:08:21,540 --> 00:08:23,800
and they just want to trample on our ancient liberties

214
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:25,000
and all of that.

215
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:26,480
And now people start to say,

216
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:28,920
well, this is all the fault of the king's ministers.

217
00:08:29,080 --> 00:08:30,080
They're not giving us any relief.

218
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:31,940
They're probably hoarding grain themselves,

219
00:08:32,220 --> 00:08:32,600
to be honest.

220
00:08:32,780 --> 00:08:34,520
They're probably profiting from all this

221
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:35,620
and driving prices up.

222
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:38,460
Only the Estates General can save us.

223
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:41,100
And then if that's not bad enough,

224
00:08:41,160 --> 00:08:45,200
I promised you that nature had more tricks up our sleeve.

225
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:48,400
On the 26th of November, 1788,

226
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:52,280
there is an unexpected snowfall.

227
00:08:52,340 --> 00:08:54,980
Basically, winter has come unexpectedly heavy.

228
00:08:54,980 --> 00:08:58,760
And this kicks off what Robert Danson describes

229
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:01,360
as the longest, cruelest, coldest winter

230
00:09:01,360 --> 00:09:03,360
that anybody in France had ever known.

231
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:07,960
So by December, Paris is completely covered with snow.

232
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:09,480
The Seine is frozen over.

233
00:09:09,560 --> 00:09:10,880
People are walking on the Seine.

234
00:09:11,840 --> 00:09:14,740
There are frozen bodies lying in the streets.

235
00:09:15,480 --> 00:09:16,740
By New Year's Eve,

236
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:19,660
the temperature has fallen to the lowest ever recorded in France,

237
00:09:19,860 --> 00:09:20,980
minus 10 degrees Fahrenheit.

238
00:09:22,420 --> 00:09:24,100
The rivers are all frozen.

239
00:09:24,180 --> 00:09:24,500
Of course,

240
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:27,000
the rivers being frozen means that the water mills...

241
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:28,160
Yeah, so they can't grind the...

242
00:09:28,160 --> 00:09:29,860
They can't create flour.

243
00:09:30,160 --> 00:09:30,420
Yeah.

244
00:09:31,440 --> 00:09:33,440
The rivers are frozen.

245
00:09:33,560 --> 00:09:35,100
The roads are snowbound.

246
00:09:35,260 --> 00:09:37,640
They cannot transport supplies of bread

247
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:39,680
to those areas that are suffering the most.

248
00:09:40,060 --> 00:09:42,020
And the snow is everywhere in France.

249
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:44,640
It's as far south as the Languedoc in the southwest.

250
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:48,440
And it falls pretty much every other day

251
00:09:48,440 --> 00:09:51,600
between February and April, 1789.

252
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:53,780
I mean, this is a real freak winter.

253
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:55,940
And so they must all be sitting,

254
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:57,420
you know, standing in Versailles,

255
00:09:57,520 --> 00:09:58,240
looking out of the window,

256
00:09:58,380 --> 00:09:59,720
thinking, oh, no.

257
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:00,420
Completely, they are.

258
00:10:00,540 --> 00:10:02,280
Yeah, because they're getting reports all the time

259
00:10:02,280 --> 00:10:04,300
that basically there's a lot of people in France

260
00:10:04,300 --> 00:10:05,420
who are labourers with no land

261
00:10:05,420 --> 00:10:07,300
and they rely on seasonal work

262
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:08,720
and they're all out of work

263
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:09,500
and they're just starving.

264
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:11,060
You know, they've no money.

265
00:10:11,100 --> 00:10:11,860
They have no food.

266
00:10:12,040 --> 00:10:12,960
And dying, presumably.

267
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:13,880
And dying, yeah.

268
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:15,660
The majority of French men and women

269
00:10:15,660 --> 00:10:17,600
are peasant sharecroppers, smallholders.

270
00:10:18,120 --> 00:10:19,540
So they work their land.

271
00:10:19,680 --> 00:10:22,400
They owe the seigneur, the local landowner,

272
00:10:22,540 --> 00:10:24,240
they owe him for goods and stuff.

273
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:26,480
And they owe him various exactions.

274
00:10:27,720 --> 00:10:30,460
With this winter following on the storm,

275
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:33,120
they probably won't be able to pay him what they owe.

276
00:10:34,020 --> 00:10:36,060
This means that they will probably be evicted

277
00:10:36,060 --> 00:10:36,700
from their land

278
00:10:36,700 --> 00:10:38,580
and they will be demoted

279
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,460
to the class of those people who have no land,

280
00:10:41,580 --> 00:10:42,480
which for them...

281
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:43,800
But at least they have the consolation

282
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:44,680
of knowing that the seigneur

283
00:10:44,680 --> 00:10:45,940
doesn't have to pay taxes.

284
00:10:46,260 --> 00:10:47,280
So that must be a relief.

285
00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:51,480
Yeah, that the seigneur is fighting

286
00:10:51,480 --> 00:10:53,080
for the liberties of France.

287
00:10:53,260 --> 00:10:54,120
Yeah, for the liberty.

288
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:55,820
For his right not to pay taxes.

289
00:10:56,140 --> 00:10:57,400
Tom, I think it's fair to say

290
00:10:57,400 --> 00:10:58,780
that on Gemma and the Rest is History,

291
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,740
we deplore people who don't pay their rightful taxes.

292
00:11:02,020 --> 00:11:02,420
Don't we?

293
00:11:02,580 --> 00:11:04,720
We set out our position a year ago

294
00:11:04,720 --> 00:11:06,440
in the series on the American War of Independence.

295
00:11:06,820 --> 00:11:07,140
We did.

296
00:11:07,140 --> 00:11:08,240
And we're sticking to it now.

297
00:11:08,420 --> 00:11:09,920
Just pay your taxes, for God's sake.

298
00:11:09,940 --> 00:11:12,280
French, American, what is wrong with you people?

299
00:11:12,280 --> 00:11:13,480
But this is such a hostage to fortune

300
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:15,200
when we get investigated by tax collectors.

301
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:15,720
That's true.

302
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:18,320
So now, anyway, you have a flood of people

303
00:11:18,820 --> 00:11:19,980
moving into the cities,

304
00:11:20,720 --> 00:11:22,040
desperate for relief.

305
00:11:22,400 --> 00:11:24,260
But if they manage to get there,

306
00:11:24,260 --> 00:11:24,840
they're through the snow.

307
00:11:24,980 --> 00:11:26,960
There is very little there for them

308
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:29,160
because France already is suffering

309
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:31,400
from quite high unemployment in the cities

310
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:33,840
because, as we said right at the beginning

311
00:11:33,840 --> 00:11:34,780
of the last episode,

312
00:11:35,720 --> 00:11:39,420
the Ancien Régime is a modernizing regime.

313
00:11:39,980 --> 00:11:41,920
We're living in the age of the Industrial Revolution.

314
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:43,900
People are being, weavers, artisans,

315
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:45,720
are being put out of work by machines,

316
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:47,520
by capitalism.

317
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:49,620
And by Britain.

318
00:11:49,700 --> 00:11:50,240
And by Britain.

319
00:11:50,260 --> 00:11:50,640
More specifically.

320
00:11:51,020 --> 00:11:51,780
Yeah, because Calon,

321
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:54,020
who wanted to liberalize the French economy,

322
00:11:54,260 --> 00:11:56,540
had signed, this is very Brexit negotiations,

323
00:11:57,060 --> 00:11:59,220
he'd signed a free trade deal with the British.

324
00:11:59,680 --> 00:12:01,740
And he'd said, this will give us the shock we need,

325
00:12:01,780 --> 00:12:02,400
a bit of competition,

326
00:12:02,680 --> 00:12:04,600
a bit of, you know, a spur to our economy,

327
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:07,980
a flood of British manufactured goods.

328
00:12:08,220 --> 00:12:09,440
Yeah, cheap gloves and things.

329
00:12:09,560 --> 00:12:12,780
Cheap gloves that are putting the French out of work.

330
00:12:13,040 --> 00:12:14,020
So over that...

331
00:12:14,020 --> 00:12:16,120
That's what my ancestors made in Derbyshire.

332
00:12:16,200 --> 00:12:16,880
They were making gloves.

333
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:17,140
Really?

334
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:20,040
So basically, your ancestors are responsible

335
00:12:20,040 --> 00:12:21,640
for the Day of the Tiles in Grenoble.

336
00:12:21,880 --> 00:12:22,400
Yeah, we played our bit.

337
00:12:22,460 --> 00:12:22,700
Wow.

338
00:12:22,780 --> 00:12:24,100
Tom, you kept that quiet during that.

339
00:12:24,100 --> 00:12:25,140
That whole incident when people,

340
00:12:25,240 --> 00:12:26,280
12-year-olds were being shot,

341
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:27,720
but now you bring it up.

342
00:12:27,780 --> 00:12:28,180
Oh, yeah.

343
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:29,900
I'd actually forgotten about it.

344
00:12:29,920 --> 00:12:30,620
Unbelievable, isn't it?

345
00:12:30,700 --> 00:12:31,100
That's the worst.

346
00:12:31,240 --> 00:12:32,080
That's even worse.

347
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:32,880
It's the indifference.

348
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:33,580
I know.

349
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:35,160
It's the indifference to the suffering you've caused.

350
00:12:36,460 --> 00:12:36,820
Right.

351
00:12:37,380 --> 00:12:38,300
I mean, you should be,

352
00:12:38,340 --> 00:12:40,660
you can do better and be true to your values, Tom, please.

353
00:12:41,700 --> 00:12:43,620
So over the winter of 1788,

354
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:48,100
about a fifth of the population of Paris are unemployed.

355
00:12:48,480 --> 00:12:49,860
So that's what, about 100,000?

356
00:12:50,420 --> 00:12:51,600
100,000 people, yeah.

357
00:12:51,880 --> 00:12:53,420
So, or, well,

358
00:12:54,100 --> 00:12:56,300
we're receiving relief of some kind, like poor relief.

359
00:12:56,800 --> 00:12:58,880
So now we have the three ingredients

360
00:12:58,880 --> 00:13:00,660
that we talked about last time.

361
00:13:00,740 --> 00:13:02,280
The political and financial crisis,

362
00:13:02,340 --> 00:13:03,220
the kind of high politics,

363
00:13:03,860 --> 00:13:05,160
the cultural moment,

364
00:13:05,420 --> 00:13:08,340
the cult of virtue, of passion,

365
00:13:09,100 --> 00:13:10,760
of, you know, living your truth,

366
00:13:10,840 --> 00:13:11,880
all of that stuff,

367
00:13:12,040 --> 00:13:13,980
and now the social unrest

368
00:13:13,980 --> 00:13:15,580
and the anger and the desperation.

369
00:13:16,600 --> 00:13:18,280
So this is a lot of pressure

370
00:13:18,900 --> 00:13:20,320
on the Estates General,

371
00:13:20,440 --> 00:13:21,180
which has been called

372
00:13:21,180 --> 00:13:23,060
and is supposed to resolve all this.

373
00:13:24,100 --> 00:13:24,540
And the thing is,

374
00:13:24,560 --> 00:13:25,700
they've called the Estates General

375
00:13:25,700 --> 00:13:28,040
and it's going to meet the following spring, summer.

376
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:30,120
The date is a slightly movable feast.

377
00:13:30,960 --> 00:13:32,880
But they don't really know, as we said,

378
00:13:33,660 --> 00:13:34,820
what it is,

379
00:13:34,900 --> 00:13:36,540
how it will be arranged.

380
00:13:38,460 --> 00:13:40,280
There's no institutional memory of it

381
00:13:40,280 --> 00:13:42,240
because it hasn't met since 1614, 15.

382
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:44,180
And the king has not decreed what it should be.

383
00:13:44,380 --> 00:13:44,500
No.

384
00:13:45,100 --> 00:13:46,660
There's a flood of pamphlets.

385
00:13:46,960 --> 00:13:49,040
There's a huge sort of torrent of argument

386
00:13:49,560 --> 00:13:51,500
in the final months of 1788.

387
00:13:52,100 --> 00:13:53,380
And what people want,

388
00:13:53,500 --> 00:13:54,000
if they are,

389
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:54,760
if they are kind of reformers,

390
00:13:54,760 --> 00:13:57,780
they say the third estate,

391
00:13:58,120 --> 00:13:59,140
which is the commons,

392
00:13:59,160 --> 00:14:00,780
representing the great majority

393
00:14:00,780 --> 00:14:01,700
of the French people,

394
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:05,340
should absolutely not allow itself

395
00:14:05,340 --> 00:14:06,980
to be outvoted by the clergy

396
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:07,940
and the nobility.

397
00:14:08,620 --> 00:14:10,060
So the third estate...

398
00:14:10,060 --> 00:14:11,080
The clergy, the first estate,

399
00:14:11,720 --> 00:14:12,880
nobles of the second estate.

400
00:14:12,980 --> 00:14:13,200
Exactly.

401
00:14:13,480 --> 00:14:14,720
The mass of everybody else

402
00:14:14,720 --> 00:14:15,340
is the third estate.

403
00:14:15,380 --> 00:14:16,100
Everybody else, yeah.

404
00:14:16,220 --> 00:14:17,680
The third estate should have

405
00:14:17,680 --> 00:14:18,460
its numbers doubled,

406
00:14:18,620 --> 00:14:19,620
so it should be equivalent

407
00:14:19,620 --> 00:14:20,300
to those two.

408
00:14:20,760 --> 00:14:22,800
And they should all sit together

409
00:14:24,180 --> 00:14:26,260
so that a majority vote

410
00:14:26,260 --> 00:14:28,340
can decide all the big questions

411
00:14:28,340 --> 00:14:29,680
that basically shouldn't be out.

412
00:14:29,860 --> 00:14:32,020
Because even if its numbers are doubled,

413
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:33,580
if it's outvoted

414
00:14:33,580 --> 00:14:34,760
by the other two chambers,

415
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:36,320
if each chamber gets basically

416
00:14:36,320 --> 00:14:37,740
one vote, as it were,

417
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:40,500
then they'll never be able to win.

418
00:14:40,820 --> 00:14:41,800
And can I ask,

419
00:14:41,820 --> 00:14:43,580
with all this talk about,

420
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:45,480
you know, the general will

421
00:14:45,480 --> 00:14:46,560
of the people,

422
00:14:47,040 --> 00:14:48,820
which is already becoming very current,

423
00:14:49,060 --> 00:14:50,560
is there already a sense

424
00:14:50,560 --> 00:14:51,960
that actually there shouldn't be

425
00:14:51,960 --> 00:14:52,520
three estates,

426
00:14:52,540 --> 00:14:53,460
there should just be the one

427
00:14:53,460 --> 00:14:55,200
national assembly

428
00:14:55,800 --> 00:14:56,800
which could speak

429
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:57,500
and articulate

430
00:14:57,500 --> 00:15:01,760
the truth of the nation?

431
00:15:02,100 --> 00:15:02,500
Yes.

432
00:15:02,700 --> 00:15:03,680
And this is the moment

433
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:04,940
when people start to say that.

434
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:06,640
So people had not really

435
00:15:06,640 --> 00:15:08,700
been saying that so openly

436
00:15:08,700 --> 00:15:10,640
in, you know, 1780 or something.

437
00:15:11,200 --> 00:15:12,740
But now by 1788,

438
00:15:12,900 --> 00:15:14,100
they're saying it very openly.

439
00:15:14,180 --> 00:15:15,440
And the most famous example of that,

440
00:15:15,500 --> 00:15:16,020
actually, Tom,

441
00:15:16,060 --> 00:15:17,180
is a guy called Siez,

442
00:15:17,300 --> 00:15:19,500
the Abbe Siez, Emmanuel Siez.

443
00:15:19,880 --> 00:15:21,620
So he is from a commoner family

444
00:15:21,620 --> 00:15:23,340
and he had become a kind of church,

445
00:15:23,460 --> 00:15:24,860
administrator in Chartres.

446
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:27,480
And he writes a very famous pamphlet.

447
00:15:27,580 --> 00:15:29,460
If anyone has ever done this

448
00:15:29,460 --> 00:15:30,440
for A-level like me,

449
00:15:30,520 --> 00:15:31,340
they will remember this.

450
00:15:31,420 --> 00:15:32,260
He wrote a pamphlet called

451
00:15:32,260 --> 00:15:34,020
Qu'est-ce que le tiers état?

452
00:15:34,100 --> 00:15:35,060
What is the third estate?

453
00:15:35,280 --> 00:15:37,100
Which came out in January 1789.

454
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:39,100
And it started with these famous words.

455
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:41,480
We have three questions to ask.

456
00:15:41,820 --> 00:15:44,020
First, what is the third estate?

457
00:15:44,640 --> 00:15:45,120
Everything.

458
00:15:46,360 --> 00:15:47,840
Second, what has it been

459
00:15:47,840 --> 00:15:49,580
in the political order up to now?

460
00:15:50,200 --> 00:15:50,680
Nothing.

461
00:15:52,060 --> 00:15:53,420
Third, what does it do?

462
00:15:53,460 --> 00:15:55,520
Demand to become something.

463
00:15:56,640 --> 00:15:57,900
So this sort of idea

464
00:15:57,900 --> 00:15:59,980
that the third estate is everything.

465
00:16:00,280 --> 00:16:01,620
And he says, Siez,

466
00:16:02,220 --> 00:16:04,880
the third estate is a complete nation.

467
00:16:05,180 --> 00:16:07,140
He says there are 25 million of us.

468
00:16:07,480 --> 00:16:09,660
There are only 200,000 of them,

469
00:16:09,740 --> 00:16:10,660
nobles and priests.

470
00:16:10,780 --> 00:16:11,740
We don't even need them.

471
00:16:12,200 --> 00:16:14,580
He says we are the nation.

472
00:16:15,260 --> 00:16:18,480
We, if we want a total reboot of France,

473
00:16:19,160 --> 00:16:20,380
we can have one.

474
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:21,960
If we lack a constitution,

475
00:16:22,260 --> 00:16:23,200
we must make one.

476
00:16:23,460 --> 00:16:26,100
The nation alone has that right.

477
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:27,900
And he, I mean, he presumably,

478
00:16:28,200 --> 00:16:29,900
I mean, he's a member of the first estate.

479
00:16:30,140 --> 00:16:30,320
Yes.

480
00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:32,320
And we talked in the previous episode

481
00:16:32,320 --> 00:16:34,600
that there are nobles, members of the second estate

482
00:16:35,140 --> 00:16:36,360
who sympathise with this.

483
00:16:36,520 --> 00:16:36,760
Yes.

484
00:16:36,840 --> 00:16:39,920
That, you know, they would like to see their order,

485
00:16:39,980 --> 00:16:44,480
their estate dissolved into the entirety of a single assembly.

486
00:16:44,740 --> 00:16:45,420
They absolutely would.

487
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:46,140
Yeah, they would.

488
00:16:46,220 --> 00:16:49,520
So we'll see a very famous example in the Comte de Mirabeau,

489
00:16:49,640 --> 00:16:51,980
who is one of the most famous spokesmen for the third estate,

490
00:16:52,140 --> 00:16:53,200
but is a nobleman.

491
00:16:53,200 --> 00:16:56,060
And, of course, also the Duke of Orléans,

492
00:16:56,220 --> 00:16:57,440
who, again, we talked to,

493
00:16:57,460 --> 00:16:59,360
the cousin of Louis XVI,

494
00:16:59,420 --> 00:17:02,660
who, you know, is a member, obviously, of the second estate.

495
00:17:02,880 --> 00:17:04,700
And actually, some of these people are very vitriolic

496
00:17:04,700 --> 00:17:05,740
about their own estates.

497
00:17:05,780 --> 00:17:06,800
So here's an example.

498
00:17:07,040 --> 00:17:08,940
There's a real hint of menace in that pamphlet,

499
00:17:09,020 --> 00:17:09,920
What is the third estate?

500
00:17:10,360 --> 00:17:11,020
He says,

501
00:17:11,700 --> 00:17:14,260
if the nobility and the clergy, like me,

502
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:16,040
want to become part of the nation,

503
00:17:16,320 --> 00:17:17,700
they must discard their privileges.

504
00:17:18,480 --> 00:17:19,220
And he says,

505
00:17:19,260 --> 00:17:21,280
people are always asking what place should they occupy

506
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:22,180
in the social order.

507
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:23,020
He says,

508
00:17:23,200 --> 00:17:25,000
this is equivalent to asking

509
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,100
what place should be assigned to a malignant disease

510
00:17:28,640 --> 00:17:32,020
which preys upon and tortures the body of a sick man.

511
00:17:32,820 --> 00:17:34,420
I mean, that analogy,

512
00:17:35,120 --> 00:17:37,760
your opponents are not even traitors.

513
00:17:37,980 --> 00:17:39,360
They are a disease.

514
00:17:39,980 --> 00:17:41,080
Yeah, it's very familiar, isn't it?

515
00:17:41,140 --> 00:17:42,700
I mean, that is very familiar and very disturbing.

516
00:17:43,400 --> 00:17:45,180
So anyway, in this argument,

517
00:17:46,140 --> 00:17:48,840
Necker suggests a compromise,

518
00:17:48,880 --> 00:17:50,300
and he and the king issue a decision.

519
00:17:50,680 --> 00:17:52,960
The third estate will get its key demand.

520
00:17:53,200 --> 00:17:54,520
It will get 600 deputies

521
00:17:54,520 --> 00:17:56,740
compared with the 300 or so

522
00:17:56,740 --> 00:17:58,880
for both the clergy and the nobility.

523
00:17:59,580 --> 00:18:02,260
Also, the third estate will be able to elect,

524
00:18:02,320 --> 00:18:03,660
choose its representatives,

525
00:18:04,020 --> 00:18:05,040
people like Siez,

526
00:18:05,540 --> 00:18:07,540
who come from the other estates.

527
00:18:07,640 --> 00:18:08,880
So if you're, Tom,

528
00:18:08,960 --> 00:18:12,060
a liberal reforming clergyman like him

529
00:18:12,060 --> 00:18:14,180
or a vicomte or whatever,

530
00:18:14,860 --> 00:18:17,160
and your own estate won't choose you,

531
00:18:17,180 --> 00:18:17,600
obviously,

532
00:18:18,160 --> 00:18:20,600
you could run in the third estate elections

533
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:21,940
to get in that way.

534
00:18:22,360 --> 00:18:22,880
However,

535
00:18:23,200 --> 00:18:25,020
what Necker does not decide

536
00:18:25,020 --> 00:18:27,000
is whether the three estates

537
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:28,020
are all going to sit together,

538
00:18:28,200 --> 00:18:30,020
which would allow the third estate to dominate,

539
00:18:30,240 --> 00:18:32,420
or whether they would still be stratified

540
00:18:32,420 --> 00:18:33,480
kind of hierarchically

541
00:18:33,480 --> 00:18:35,980
and be in their different chambers.

542
00:18:37,280 --> 00:18:38,820
So this kicks off

543
00:18:39,840 --> 00:18:42,380
these elections for the three estates,

544
00:18:42,420 --> 00:18:43,520
and they're very, very complicated.

545
00:18:43,600 --> 00:18:45,860
So let's try to stay clear of them

546
00:18:45,860 --> 00:18:46,660
as much as possible.

547
00:18:47,020 --> 00:18:48,940
Basically, it's a filtration process.

548
00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:51,340
You've got a parish assembly

549
00:18:51,340 --> 00:18:52,760
and then a district assembly,

550
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:54,040
and then a provincial assembly.

551
00:18:54,100 --> 00:18:55,060
It kind of varies from place to place, doesn't it?

552
00:18:55,060 --> 00:18:55,240
It does.

553
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:56,280
It's all kind of random.

554
00:18:56,520 --> 00:18:57,620
Yeah, very complicated.

555
00:18:57,620 --> 00:18:58,220
And all the time,

556
00:18:58,220 --> 00:18:59,340
people have been encouraged.

557
00:18:59,640 --> 00:19:00,760
And this is an amazing thing.

558
00:19:00,900 --> 00:19:01,800
The king says to them,

559
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:04,220
I want to hear all your grievances.

560
00:19:04,820 --> 00:19:07,840
I want to hear all your desires.

561
00:19:08,240 --> 00:19:08,820
So actually,

562
00:19:08,920 --> 00:19:10,980
the idea that the elite and the regime

563
00:19:10,980 --> 00:19:12,940
is totally indifferent and out of touch

564
00:19:12,940 --> 00:19:13,760
is not quite right.

565
00:19:14,020 --> 00:19:14,680
But this is the king.

566
00:19:14,780 --> 00:19:16,760
This is him playing his role of the father.

567
00:19:16,800 --> 00:19:18,100
Yeah, the father of the nation.

568
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:19,340
Because, of course,

569
00:19:19,360 --> 00:19:21,180
I assume at this point,

570
00:19:21,800 --> 00:19:23,300
the implications of all this

571
00:19:23,760 --> 00:19:25,740
identification of the third estate

572
00:19:25,740 --> 00:19:27,760
with the voice of the people,

573
00:19:28,260 --> 00:19:29,480
they're not yet discussing

574
00:19:29,480 --> 00:19:30,700
what implications that might have

575
00:19:30,700 --> 00:19:31,380
for the monarch.

576
00:19:31,420 --> 00:19:32,480
No, because they're all,

577
00:19:32,540 --> 00:19:33,060
by the way,

578
00:19:33,100 --> 00:19:33,980
they're all monarchists.

579
00:19:34,320 --> 00:19:34,940
I mean,

580
00:19:34,980 --> 00:19:35,360
Of course.

581
00:19:35,680 --> 00:19:37,160
I mean, that's taken for granted.

582
00:19:37,360 --> 00:19:38,840
There is very little of a,

583
00:19:39,400 --> 00:19:40,020
I mean,

584
00:19:40,020 --> 00:19:41,840
certainly in the books that I've read,

585
00:19:41,880 --> 00:19:43,820
there's no mention of any of a republicanism

586
00:19:43,820 --> 00:19:44,920
at this stage.

587
00:19:45,060 --> 00:19:46,400
Their dream, remember,

588
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:47,400
a lot of these reformers

589
00:19:47,400 --> 00:19:49,080
is a British-style system,

590
00:19:49,560 --> 00:19:51,160
not an American republicanism.

591
00:19:52,160 --> 00:19:53,840
So the ordinary people

592
00:19:53,840 --> 00:19:56,940
are composing these 25,000 dossiers,

593
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:00,500
they could call it exercise books,

594
00:20:00,660 --> 00:20:01,320
you could call them,

595
00:20:02,980 --> 00:20:04,780
in which they basically are saying,

596
00:20:04,900 --> 00:20:05,940
well, I don't like this tax.

597
00:20:06,140 --> 00:20:07,940
I don't like the senior does this.

598
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:11,420
I would love to live with this or that

599
00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:11,840
or whatever.

600
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:13,080
This is too many hailstones.

601
00:20:13,080 --> 00:20:14,740
But no one in Europe has ever,

602
00:20:14,780 --> 00:20:15,960
I think possibly in the world,

603
00:20:16,020 --> 00:20:18,740
has ever done an exercise like this before.

604
00:20:18,800 --> 00:20:21,020
It is a monumental sort of,

605
00:20:21,180 --> 00:20:22,820
public opinion survey.

606
00:20:23,040 --> 00:20:24,560
And does it help stabilise the situation?

607
00:20:24,780 --> 00:20:26,020
Well, obviously the problem with it

608
00:20:26,020 --> 00:20:28,300
is that it raises expectations that no,

609
00:20:28,420 --> 00:20:30,160
because it's unprecedented and extraordinary,

610
00:20:30,440 --> 00:20:32,360
it raises expectations no government,

611
00:20:32,940 --> 00:20:34,300
even a revolutionary government,

612
00:20:34,560 --> 00:20:36,100
can possibly meet

613
00:20:36,100 --> 00:20:38,300
because people then, as now,

614
00:20:39,040 --> 00:20:41,360
they want impossible and contradictory things.

615
00:20:41,560 --> 00:20:43,040
They want more food.

616
00:20:43,220 --> 00:20:44,460
They want more freedom.

617
00:20:44,640 --> 00:20:46,020
They want lower prices.

618
00:20:46,420 --> 00:20:49,240
They want less government on their backs

619
00:20:49,240 --> 00:20:50,220
and telling them what to do.

620
00:20:50,280 --> 00:20:51,160
They also want,

621
00:20:51,180 --> 00:20:51,960
they want more government,

622
00:20:52,160 --> 00:20:54,220
a paternalistic government that is giving them,

623
00:20:54,260 --> 00:20:55,460
that was always there with relief

624
00:20:55,460 --> 00:20:56,660
and all this kind of thing.

625
00:20:56,780 --> 00:20:59,360
They're asking things that no 18th century government

626
00:20:59,360 --> 00:21:03,500
can possibly supply.

627
00:21:03,860 --> 00:21:05,020
And that will create,

628
00:21:05,160 --> 00:21:07,340
that's stoking their expectations to a pitch

629
00:21:07,340 --> 00:21:10,460
where the dissatisfaction will explode

630
00:21:10,460 --> 00:21:14,340
in fury and disappointment very shortly.

631
00:21:15,380 --> 00:21:15,900
Meanwhile,

632
00:21:16,080 --> 00:21:18,580
even as they are electing their representatives

633
00:21:18,580 --> 00:21:19,900
and drawing up their CAE,

634
00:21:20,640 --> 00:21:21,160
they're asking things like,

635
00:21:21,180 --> 00:21:22,380
well, what's the point of this?

636
00:21:22,520 --> 00:21:23,140
And that's the point of the revolution.

637
00:21:23,140 --> 00:21:23,320
And that's the point of the revolution.

638
00:21:23,380 --> 00:21:23,480
And again,

639
00:21:24,360 --> 00:21:26,120
this doesn't start with the revolution.

640
00:21:26,380 --> 00:21:27,760
It precedes the revolution.

641
00:21:28,160 --> 00:21:29,180
So effectively,

642
00:21:29,420 --> 00:21:31,680
I think what happens is the snow melts

643
00:21:31,680 --> 00:21:33,320
as the ice thaws

644
00:21:33,320 --> 00:21:35,160
and people go back to the streets

645
00:21:35,160 --> 00:21:38,700
and the food prices are still very high.

646
00:21:39,160 --> 00:21:42,420
So the disorder returns to the streets

647
00:21:42,420 --> 00:21:44,320
and you get a series of reports

648
00:21:44,320 --> 00:21:48,280
by about March of food rioting across the country,

649
00:21:48,340 --> 00:21:50,440
all the way from France in the north

650
00:21:50,440 --> 00:21:51,080
to Avignon,

651
00:21:51,180 --> 00:21:53,460
to Toulouse in the south.

652
00:21:54,080 --> 00:21:55,300
And in particular,

653
00:21:55,460 --> 00:21:57,780
there is a sense that in the countryside,

654
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,280
which is most of France,

655
00:22:01,880 --> 00:22:04,540
authority is really breaking down at this point.

656
00:22:04,720 --> 00:22:07,440
So I know we've both read Sharma's book.

657
00:22:07,640 --> 00:22:09,620
He has this wonderful section, doesn't he,

658
00:22:09,640 --> 00:22:11,040
where he introduced this by saying

659
00:22:11,040 --> 00:22:12,880
the first mass casualties,

660
00:22:12,960 --> 00:22:14,760
the French Revolution of France is rabbits.

661
00:22:15,420 --> 00:22:17,900
Because he describes how people just,

662
00:22:18,820 --> 00:22:19,940
they're desperate.

663
00:22:20,060 --> 00:22:21,080
They form platoons,

664
00:22:21,720 --> 00:22:23,760
and they go out with guns or whatever,

665
00:22:23,940 --> 00:22:25,660
or kind of bludgeons,

666
00:22:26,260 --> 00:22:27,820
like British schoolboys.

667
00:22:28,300 --> 00:22:30,940
And they're hunting game and rabbits

668
00:22:30,940 --> 00:22:32,160
and all this sort of stuff.

669
00:22:32,340 --> 00:22:33,520
And this is in contravention

670
00:22:33,520 --> 00:22:36,100
of very restrictive game laws.

671
00:22:36,400 --> 00:22:36,660
Yeah.

672
00:22:36,760 --> 00:22:37,860
And actually,

673
00:22:37,940 --> 00:22:40,300
it's one of the stories of 1789

674
00:22:40,300 --> 00:22:42,780
is the erosion of those game laws.

675
00:22:43,280 --> 00:22:45,260
And it's bad news for rabbits.

676
00:22:45,320 --> 00:22:46,900
And in a very short time,

677
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:48,480
it will be bad news for birds.

678
00:22:48,600 --> 00:22:50,480
Yeah, because when gamekeepers

679
00:22:50,480 --> 00:22:51,160
try to stop them,

680
00:22:51,160 --> 00:22:52,100
and say,

681
00:22:52,200 --> 00:22:54,100
oh, this is the seniors stuff,

682
00:22:54,780 --> 00:22:57,460
they're just shooting them dead and continue.

683
00:22:58,140 --> 00:23:00,220
But you think the real victims in this are the birds,

684
00:23:00,260 --> 00:23:00,600
do you, Tom?

685
00:23:00,700 --> 00:23:01,160
I do, really,

686
00:23:01,280 --> 00:23:02,860
because they've remained victims,

687
00:23:03,440 --> 00:23:05,600
because it becomes a kind of principle of,

688
00:23:06,220 --> 00:23:06,780
you know,

689
00:23:06,800 --> 00:23:07,660
the new order in France,

690
00:23:07,780 --> 00:23:09,140
that you can kill as many birds as you like.

691
00:23:09,240 --> 00:23:11,480
Like François Mitterrand eating that bird on his deathbed.

692
00:23:11,660 --> 00:23:12,080
Absolutely.

693
00:23:12,340 --> 00:23:12,560
Yeah.

694
00:23:12,620 --> 00:23:12,820
Yeah.

695
00:23:12,880 --> 00:23:13,180
Unbelievable.

696
00:23:13,420 --> 00:23:13,640
Yeah.

697
00:23:13,740 --> 00:23:18,340
It's a slippery slope from bread riots to François Mitterrand

698
00:23:18,340 --> 00:23:20,400
biting the head off an innocent bird.

699
00:23:20,400 --> 00:23:21,880
Oh, no, what a terrible image.

700
00:23:22,740 --> 00:23:25,160
So there's a real sense of a power vacuum in the countryside.

701
00:23:25,520 --> 00:23:27,680
People are attacking granaries and bakeries.

702
00:23:28,540 --> 00:23:31,980
Towns unable to defend themselves are forming their own militias.

703
00:23:33,220 --> 00:23:34,520
Again, that's not good.

704
00:23:34,660 --> 00:23:34,840
Yeah.

705
00:23:34,900 --> 00:23:36,540
It's all kind of starting to crumble.

706
00:23:36,700 --> 00:23:39,040
But you know what you need in a situation like this?

707
00:23:39,120 --> 00:23:39,320
Tell me.

708
00:23:39,420 --> 00:23:40,820
You need a massive riot.

709
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:41,400
Yes.

710
00:23:41,800 --> 00:23:43,300
Dominic, does such a thing happen?

711
00:23:43,500 --> 00:23:43,820
It does.

712
00:23:43,940 --> 00:23:44,840
So it happens in Paris.

713
00:23:44,960 --> 00:23:45,800
And I think, again,

714
00:23:46,440 --> 00:23:50,280
I said in the last episode that the French Revolution started in Grenoble.

715
00:23:50,400 --> 00:23:53,860
You could also say, I mean, if you want to choose an alternative date,

716
00:23:53,980 --> 00:23:58,840
it starts on the 28th of April 1789 in Paris, not at the Bastille.

717
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:02,640
This starts in a place east of the Bastille called the Faubourg,

718
00:24:02,640 --> 00:24:05,420
that means neighborhood, Saint-Antoine, Saint-Anthony.

719
00:24:06,060 --> 00:24:07,980
And there's a rumor that goes around.

720
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:12,940
There's a big wallpaper manufacturer in town called Réveillon.

721
00:24:13,260 --> 00:24:17,700
And there's a rumor that Réveillon had gone to a public meeting and had said

722
00:24:17,700 --> 00:24:20,380
that his workers should live on 15 days.

723
00:24:20,380 --> 00:24:26,300
15 sous a day, the price of which is now the price of a loaf of bread, one loaf only.

724
00:24:27,020 --> 00:24:28,940
And actually, he hadn't said that.

725
00:24:29,060 --> 00:24:33,920
What he had said was that if they sorted out the economy and liberalized the economy,

726
00:24:34,160 --> 00:24:38,920
there would be lower prices and then with lower prices, there would be lower wages.

727
00:24:39,160 --> 00:24:44,320
And one day his workers would live on 15 sous a day because bread would be so cheap.

728
00:24:44,580 --> 00:24:45,840
Or words to that effect.

729
00:24:46,720 --> 00:24:49,140
Now, Réveillon is very well known in Paris.

730
00:24:49,840 --> 00:24:53,340
He had made, in his capacity as a paper manufacturer,

731
00:24:54,060 --> 00:25:00,200
Tom, he had made the paper that was the first balloon, the Montgolfier balloon.

732
00:25:01,260 --> 00:25:06,700
And he had sponsored the first hot air balloons and they had taken off from the yard of his workshop.

733
00:25:07,360 --> 00:25:11,420
So, you know, you will see his name if you Google the first hot air balloon flights.

734
00:25:11,500 --> 00:25:14,120
He is like a really modern, interesting person.

735
00:25:14,280 --> 00:25:15,420
Kind of James Dyson figure.

736
00:25:15,520 --> 00:25:16,020
Yeah, exactly.

737
00:25:17,320 --> 00:25:19,120
Entrepreneur, technology, the future.

738
00:25:19,140 --> 00:25:22,160
He's a really impressive person, this guy, Réveillon.

739
00:25:22,420 --> 00:25:25,200
He had been an apprentice paper worker.

740
00:25:25,620 --> 00:25:27,060
He had worked his way up.

741
00:25:27,080 --> 00:25:28,420
He had bought his own paper workshop.

742
00:25:29,600 --> 00:25:32,900
By the 1780s, he was employing hundreds of workers.

743
00:25:33,200 --> 00:25:35,460
He's brilliant at making wallpaper and paper generally.

744
00:25:35,700 --> 00:25:40,880
He's so good, he exports it to England, which, of course, given that England is the sort of…

745
00:25:40,880 --> 00:25:41,560
Yeah, Colston, Newcastle.

746
00:25:41,560 --> 00:25:42,560
Yeah, it's Colston, Newcastle.

747
00:25:42,580 --> 00:25:42,760
Exactly.

748
00:25:43,360 --> 00:25:45,560
He's also a very paternalistic employer.

749
00:25:45,740 --> 00:25:48,740
He pays his workers well more than his competitors.

750
00:25:49,140 --> 00:25:53,100
And he even pays them over that winter when it's so cold they can't work.

751
00:25:53,240 --> 00:25:54,240
He says, I'll sort you out.

752
00:25:54,600 --> 00:25:56,920
But because he's a capitalist…

753
00:25:56,920 --> 00:25:57,520
He's a saint.

754
00:25:57,920 --> 00:26:00,100
A saintly figure.

755
00:26:00,260 --> 00:26:00,960
A wonderful man.

756
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:01,920
Like the end of a Dickens novel.

757
00:26:02,040 --> 00:26:02,780
A lovely man.

758
00:26:03,020 --> 00:26:05,700
Like Mr. Cheerable or something.

759
00:26:05,980 --> 00:26:06,280
Yeah.

760
00:26:06,660 --> 00:26:13,540
Anyway, because he's a capitalist entrepreneur, basically everybody hates him because he represents a threat.

761
00:26:13,600 --> 00:26:15,840
He represents threatening modernity.

762
00:26:15,840 --> 00:26:19,120
Putting sort of craftspeople out of work.

763
00:26:19,120 --> 00:26:20,840
Like his rivals out of work.

764
00:26:21,300 --> 00:26:30,420
And so as this story spreads, people in the neighbourhood say, yeah, let's go and smash up Réveillon's workshop.

765
00:26:30,780 --> 00:26:36,120
The first day they tried to do this, some of the local sort of politicians managed to turn them away.

766
00:26:36,320 --> 00:26:42,420
But the second day, the 28th of April, the crowd is swelled by unemployed dockers and brewers and tanners and things.

767
00:26:42,700 --> 00:26:46,080
And about 5,000 to 10,000 people turn up outside Réveillon's.

768
00:26:46,080 --> 00:26:49,100
And do you think this is partly like in the election episode?

769
00:26:49,100 --> 00:26:50,540
We did a few weeks back.

770
00:26:50,540 --> 00:26:50,640
Ruffs.

771
00:26:51,040 --> 00:26:53,440
People, you know, they're told there's going to be a Barney.

772
00:26:53,480 --> 00:26:53,720
Oh, yeah.

773
00:26:54,200 --> 00:26:54,600
Completely.

774
00:26:54,920 --> 00:26:55,700
Turn up for the fun of it.

775
00:26:55,800 --> 00:26:55,820
Yeah.

776
00:26:55,820 --> 00:27:06,920
But the thing about people hearing there's going to be a Barney and turning up to see the fun of it or to join in is key to all riots and revolutions, I think.

777
00:27:08,200 --> 00:27:13,400
So all these people turn up and they basically they lay siege to his house and his workshop.

778
00:27:13,620 --> 00:27:15,240
He and his family managed to get away.

779
00:27:16,440 --> 00:27:17,840
What then happens is extraordinary.

780
00:27:17,980 --> 00:27:18,920
It takes these people.

781
00:27:18,920 --> 00:27:25,260
Literally two hours to physically destroy every trace of his house and workshop.

782
00:27:25,460 --> 00:27:27,280
They pile up literally everything.

783
00:27:27,620 --> 00:27:32,900
And I quote the furniture, the woodwork, the doors, the linens, the stocks of paper, the carriages, even some poultry.

784
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:34,920
And they set it on fire.

785
00:27:35,100 --> 00:27:37,200
So we've had rabbits and now chickens.

786
00:27:37,500 --> 00:27:41,860
The captain from Strasbourg of the guard who came to try and sort of sort out the situation.

787
00:27:42,060 --> 00:27:48,680
He said he described 15 or 1600 of the excrement of the nation vomiting up brandy.

788
00:27:48,680 --> 00:27:51,340
And presenting the most disgusting and revolting sight.

789
00:27:51,520 --> 00:27:54,760
Because these people have found what they think is Reveillon's cellar.

790
00:27:57,140 --> 00:27:59,840
Unfortunately, what they thought was his wine cellar.

791
00:27:59,860 --> 00:28:03,300
He had had thousands of bottles of very good wine that they left untouched.

792
00:28:03,520 --> 00:28:10,600
What they thought was the cellar of his wine is actually where he's storing the liquid they used to make the wallpaper.

793
00:28:11,040 --> 00:28:14,740
So loads of these blokes drink this liquid and they're found dead the next day.

794
00:28:14,880 --> 00:28:18,660
So you can see why the French Republic celebrates him.

795
00:28:18,680 --> 00:28:23,360
They celebrate the anniversary of the French Revolution on the 14th of July rather than on the 27th of April with this episode.

796
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:25,760
The excrement of the nation vomiting up brandy.

797
00:28:26,700 --> 00:28:27,980
Vomiting up paint stripper.

798
00:28:28,280 --> 00:28:28,540
Exactly.

799
00:28:29,500 --> 00:28:33,060
The city guards arrive too late to sort this out.

800
00:28:33,200 --> 00:28:37,220
As in Grenoble, they're pelted with tiles and stones by the crowd.

801
00:28:37,400 --> 00:28:40,540
And then they basically lose it and they start shooting into the crowd.

802
00:28:40,940 --> 00:28:43,060
And estimates differ massively.

803
00:28:43,540 --> 00:28:46,360
But some estimates are that 300 people.

804
00:28:46,480 --> 00:28:47,780
That's about the middle ground.

805
00:28:47,780 --> 00:28:49,820
About 300 people were killed.

806
00:28:50,120 --> 00:28:54,920
And that gives you the bloodiest day of the French Revolution until 1792.

807
00:28:56,120 --> 00:28:57,000
Didn't even happen.

808
00:28:57,080 --> 00:28:58,080
So that's the September Massacre.

809
00:28:58,080 --> 00:28:58,600
Yeah, exactly.

810
00:28:58,880 --> 00:29:02,180
I mean, this is a really, really serious business.

811
00:29:02,540 --> 00:29:07,240
But of course, completely eclipsed and forgotten now because so much worse is to follow.

812
00:29:07,520 --> 00:29:17,120
Well, and I suppose because the main event of the year is looming, or at least the scheduled main event, which is the meeting of the Estates General.

813
00:29:17,180 --> 00:29:17,420
Indeed.

814
00:29:17,780 --> 00:29:18,280
In Versailles.

815
00:29:18,460 --> 00:29:22,740
So after the Réveillon riot, Paris becomes basically an occupied city.

816
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:24,020
Troops are sent in.

817
00:29:24,320 --> 00:29:28,120
There are infantry patrols on every street.

818
00:29:28,340 --> 00:29:30,040
There are sentries on the street corners.

819
00:29:30,160 --> 00:29:31,940
There's cavalry riding up and down the streets.

820
00:29:32,180 --> 00:29:37,140
And to the horror of the Parisians, many of these troops are people who have been specially brought in.

821
00:29:37,440 --> 00:29:41,360
They are the French armies, German and Hungarian detachments.

822
00:29:41,380 --> 00:29:44,340
Basically, of course, in the 18th century, you often recruited troops from elsewhere.

823
00:29:44,800 --> 00:29:47,620
As the British did in America.

824
00:29:47,780 --> 00:29:48,660
As the British did the Hessians.

825
00:29:48,860 --> 00:29:54,980
So to have foreign troops on the streets of Paris for ordinary Parisians is very, very unsettling.

826
00:29:55,240 --> 00:29:57,220
And there are all kinds of rumours.

827
00:29:57,260 --> 00:29:58,060
There's going to be a coup.

828
00:29:58,280 --> 00:29:59,480
There's going to be an insurrection.

829
00:30:00,320 --> 00:30:01,680
Something is coming.

830
00:30:02,100 --> 00:30:08,760
And then, as you said, Tom, at the beginning of May, the Estates General opens in Versailles.

831
00:30:09,440 --> 00:30:15,120
It's all kicking off at Versailles, where the Estates General are meeting up.

832
00:30:16,040 --> 00:30:17,320
And, Dominic, what happens?

833
00:30:17,460 --> 00:30:18,520
Is it all sweetness and light?

834
00:30:18,520 --> 00:30:18,620
Sure.

835
00:30:18,620 --> 00:30:19,440
It's all fashion, Tom.

836
00:30:19,600 --> 00:30:20,400
You love a history.

837
00:30:20,640 --> 00:30:21,500
Yeah, I do.

838
00:30:21,560 --> 00:30:22,020
I love a hat.

839
00:30:22,080 --> 00:30:22,200
Yeah.

840
00:30:22,220 --> 00:30:23,060
It's all fashion.

841
00:30:23,380 --> 00:30:29,180
So in Simon Sharma's book Citizens, he starts this section with a guy called the Marquis de Ferrières.

842
00:30:30,420 --> 00:30:33,880
So he's a lovely French Revolution character.

843
00:30:34,480 --> 00:30:38,520
He wrote letters and stuff all through the Revolution, so we know what he was thinking.

844
00:30:39,820 --> 00:30:45,000
He's a very cultivated, reasonable, public-spirited man from the nobility, from Poitou.

845
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:46,060
So does he survive it?

846
00:30:46,140 --> 00:30:47,720
He does survive it, yes, which is nice.

847
00:30:49,080 --> 00:30:50,220
He's a man of the Enlightenment.

848
00:30:50,420 --> 00:30:56,880
So he's kind of looking forward to this, and he thinks, brilliant, we're going to sort out France's problems.

849
00:30:57,120 --> 00:30:59,440
He thinks, brilliant, we're going to sort out France's problems.

850
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:07,920
But when he arrives in Versailles, what is on his mind is what he is going to wear, because this is not a minor thing.

851
00:31:08,120 --> 00:31:08,740
Of course not.

852
00:31:08,780 --> 00:31:12,920
This is an important part of the ritual and the politics of the Estates General.

853
00:31:13,960 --> 00:31:17,640
Louis has been giving this some thought, as has his courtiers.

854
00:31:18,320 --> 00:31:21,400
He's very anxious about this whole Estates General thing.

855
00:31:21,480 --> 00:31:24,140
His queen, Marie Antoinette, thinks this is terrible.

856
00:31:24,420 --> 00:31:25,000
She's very good at this.

857
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:25,860
She thinks this is madness.

858
00:31:26,340 --> 00:31:27,320
She doesn't like Necker either.

859
00:31:27,440 --> 00:31:27,980
She hates Necker.

860
00:31:28,160 --> 00:31:29,580
She hates the idea of the Estates General.

861
00:31:30,060 --> 00:31:32,140
I mean, she's not wrong.

862
00:31:32,340 --> 00:31:37,680
She thinks, opening the door to this, who knows where it will lead.

863
00:31:37,880 --> 00:31:40,820
I mean, she's not untrue in her political judgments by this point.

864
00:31:40,820 --> 00:31:41,100
No.

865
00:31:41,100 --> 00:31:42,120
And Louis is very depressed.

866
00:31:42,300 --> 00:31:44,540
He's been spending a lot of time messing around with his locks and stuff.

867
00:31:44,800 --> 00:31:50,420
And to run the whole thing, he thinks the ideal person would be this guy who's 23 years old.

868
00:31:50,720 --> 00:31:53,600
He's called the Marquis de Trebreze.

869
00:31:54,580 --> 00:31:57,120
And he doesn't know anything about politics, but that's fine.

870
00:31:57,260 --> 00:31:58,780
He's really interested in court dress.

871
00:31:59,740 --> 00:32:10,220
And he and Louis decide that they need to, in the face of this instability, they need to sort of reinforce a sense of tradition and rootedness.

872
00:32:10,220 --> 00:32:14,700
So he's like the guy that you love on Twitter, who's a big fan of the King of State.

873
00:32:14,700 --> 00:32:16,580
Derek Guy, the menswear expert.

874
00:32:16,600 --> 00:32:18,560
Yeah, the guy who, the way he wears his suits.

875
00:32:18,680 --> 00:32:20,560
He would be a perfect person to run the Estates General.

876
00:32:20,980 --> 00:32:21,420
Absolutely.

877
00:32:22,460 --> 00:32:26,640
So the Marquis and Louis decide that the nobles will dress.

878
00:32:26,720 --> 00:32:28,020
They must dress up.

879
00:32:28,300 --> 00:32:33,140
They must wear gold trimmed cloaks, black silk coats, lace cravats.

880
00:32:33,260 --> 00:32:36,980
And they have these special hats, which are made of beaver with white plumes.

881
00:32:37,220 --> 00:32:39,600
I mean, beaver fur, not kind of literally.

882
00:32:39,720 --> 00:32:39,960
No.

883
00:32:40,220 --> 00:32:41,140
No, beaver fur.

884
00:32:41,280 --> 00:32:41,560
Exactly.

885
00:32:41,780 --> 00:32:42,420
Because that would be horrible.

886
00:32:42,720 --> 00:32:43,200
Yeah, it would be horrible.

887
00:32:43,340 --> 00:32:43,640
You're right.

888
00:32:44,080 --> 00:32:45,340
Disemboweled beaver on their heads.

889
00:32:45,360 --> 00:32:46,460
Of course, that'd be ridiculous, Tom.

890
00:32:46,600 --> 00:32:47,180
Let's be frank.

891
00:32:47,380 --> 00:32:48,020
It would be absurd.

892
00:32:49,400 --> 00:32:56,480
Supposedly, these beaver fur hats are the same hats that were worn in the reign of Henry IV of France.

893
00:32:56,740 --> 00:32:57,760
Who's a great hero, isn't he?

894
00:32:57,800 --> 00:32:58,600
He's the people's king.

895
00:32:58,900 --> 00:32:59,860
He's the people's king.

896
00:33:00,140 --> 00:33:01,860
Now, actually, this is kind of made up.

897
00:33:01,940 --> 00:33:06,540
No one really knows what hats they wore, but they basically invented a tradition.

898
00:33:06,820 --> 00:33:08,160
So much of the French Revolution.

899
00:33:08,400 --> 00:33:09,760
Well, you never get that in the House of Lords.

900
00:33:09,760 --> 00:33:10,240
Yeah.

901
00:33:10,360 --> 00:33:11,360
Thank God for that.

902
00:33:11,520 --> 00:33:14,080
So, the Marquis de Ferrier gets his cloak and his hat.

903
00:33:14,500 --> 00:33:19,220
And on the 4th of May, he joins all the other courtiers and they do a procession with the

904
00:33:19,220 --> 00:33:24,980
king and queen through Versailles to the Church of Saint-Louis for the formal kind of, for

905
00:33:24,980 --> 00:33:28,320
the blessing, basically, for the church service that's going to kick the whole thing off.

906
00:33:28,880 --> 00:33:31,440
And it could not be a more sumptuous scene.

907
00:33:31,540 --> 00:33:35,620
The streets are literally hung with tapestries, sort of draped over the houses.

908
00:33:35,720 --> 00:33:36,820
Gobelins tapestries.

909
00:33:37,140 --> 00:33:39,220
There are big crowds cheering long.

910
00:33:39,220 --> 00:33:41,820
I mean, they are cheering Marie Antoinette and the king.

911
00:33:41,900 --> 00:33:44,300
They shout, long live the king.

912
00:33:44,520 --> 00:33:49,860
And so, it's a procession that visualizes notions of hierarchy.

913
00:33:49,920 --> 00:33:50,360
It does.

914
00:33:50,360 --> 00:33:50,700
And actually.

915
00:33:50,860 --> 00:33:51,480
The monarchy.

916
00:33:51,660 --> 00:33:52,360
The nobility.

917
00:33:52,360 --> 00:33:52,860
It does indeed.

918
00:33:52,980 --> 00:33:53,660
The clergy.

919
00:33:55,020 --> 00:33:56,700
The general mass of the people.

920
00:33:56,800 --> 00:33:58,160
It's a bad message to send.

921
00:33:58,500 --> 00:33:59,560
It's the wrong message.

922
00:33:59,720 --> 00:34:03,660
And actually, Necker had said, could we not have the Estates General in Paris?

923
00:34:04,480 --> 00:34:08,860
Because he wanted to get it away from the court for precisely this reason.

924
00:34:08,860 --> 00:34:10,840
To avoid giving that impression.

925
00:34:12,660 --> 00:34:13,260
But no.

926
00:34:13,360 --> 00:34:15,260
They have it in Versailles.

927
00:34:15,360 --> 00:34:16,220
They have all their finery.

928
00:34:16,320 --> 00:34:19,960
And then come the third estate kind of trailing behind all the commons.

929
00:34:20,340 --> 00:34:24,340
And they have been forbidden from dressing up in finery.

930
00:34:24,400 --> 00:34:26,500
And they have been told, you must wear plain black coats.

931
00:34:27,200 --> 00:34:29,860
So, Sharma says they look like a convention of apothecaries.

932
00:34:30,480 --> 00:34:33,600
Because they look very kind of sort of solemn.

933
00:34:33,740 --> 00:34:36,840
And they look like Puritans from the Ladybird book about the English Civil War.

934
00:34:37,060 --> 00:34:37,280
Yeah.

935
00:34:37,280 --> 00:34:40,720
Kind of Dutch burgers in a Rembrandt painting.

936
00:34:40,920 --> 00:34:44,180
But there's one of them in particular who seizes the limelight.

937
00:34:44,800 --> 00:34:46,680
Sharma describes him as, and I quote,

938
00:34:46,860 --> 00:34:51,400
a mountain of flesh and muscle crammed with difficulty into black coat and hose.

939
00:34:51,700 --> 00:34:56,600
His huge face seemed to have been formed by some volcanic eruption that had cooled,

940
00:34:56,800 --> 00:35:03,080
possibly temporarily, into a crust of pumice pitted with dark holes, scabs, and craters.

941
00:35:03,360 --> 00:35:07,260
A force of nature, pagan, dangerous, and uncontrollable.

942
00:35:07,280 --> 00:35:09,400
Within clothes or custom.

943
00:35:09,460 --> 00:35:11,380
So, more bad skin action.

944
00:35:11,400 --> 00:35:11,640
Yeah.

945
00:35:11,640 --> 00:35:13,620
That's the key to the French Revolution, isn't it?

946
00:35:13,620 --> 00:35:14,340
Bad skin.

947
00:35:14,920 --> 00:35:19,940
And this monstrous figure is a fellow who, I imagine you would really like this.

948
00:35:20,020 --> 00:35:21,720
Because you like Lord Byron and you like bad boys.

949
00:35:22,500 --> 00:35:26,660
He's Honoré Gabriel Riquetti, Comte de Mirabeau.

950
00:35:26,840 --> 00:35:34,860
So, I would say that the history of the French Revolution could be written as being about Gérard Depardieu characters.

951
00:35:35,440 --> 00:35:35,680
Yeah.

952
00:35:36,400 --> 00:35:38,720
So, Danton would be another example.

953
00:35:39,660 --> 00:35:40,100
Yeah.

954
00:35:40,100 --> 00:35:42,060
This is a man who would be played by Gérard Depardieu.

955
00:35:42,100 --> 00:35:42,540
Totally.

956
00:35:42,680 --> 00:35:44,000
I suppose it hasn't happened.

957
00:35:44,200 --> 00:35:47,800
And alternated with very thin, sinister-looking people.

958
00:35:47,880 --> 00:35:50,000
Who were Robespierre and people of that ilk.

959
00:35:50,480 --> 00:35:51,300
Yeah, completely.

960
00:35:51,600 --> 00:35:52,940
So, Mirabeau is such a...

961
00:35:52,940 --> 00:35:55,480
When I did this for A-Level, he seemed such a dull dog.

962
00:35:55,760 --> 00:35:57,260
Always talking about, like...

963
00:35:57,260 --> 00:35:58,020
Constitutions.

964
00:35:59,060 --> 00:35:59,500
Electoral...

965
00:35:59,500 --> 00:36:01,240
Yeah, constitutional procedures and stuff.

966
00:36:01,420 --> 00:36:05,240
I mean, I just couldn't wait to get past Mirabeau and onto Robespierre and Marat and stuff.

967
00:36:05,240 --> 00:36:08,380
But actually, he's a tremendously interesting character.

968
00:36:08,960 --> 00:36:13,140
He's a really badly behaved, sort of posh wastrel.

969
00:36:13,260 --> 00:36:15,720
But he's also, Dominic, he's like your hero, Stanley Baldwin.

970
00:36:15,920 --> 00:36:16,100
Yeah.

971
00:36:16,120 --> 00:36:18,200
Because he's an enthusiast for writing pornography.

972
00:36:18,380 --> 00:36:18,620
Yes.

973
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:24,240
I would imagine Stanley Baldwin's work was a lot more tame than Mirabeau's.

974
00:36:24,980 --> 00:36:27,660
So, Mirabeau was a great philanderer, which Stanley Baldwin was not.

975
00:36:27,840 --> 00:36:28,960
He was very profligate.

976
00:36:28,960 --> 00:36:30,100
Again, a big contrast.

977
00:36:30,940 --> 00:36:32,960
His father had him put in prison.

978
00:36:33,340 --> 00:36:35,080
His wife took him to court.

979
00:36:35,240 --> 00:36:36,560
So, that's quite Marquis de Sade-like.

980
00:36:36,560 --> 00:36:38,460
He's very Marquis de Sade-like, actually.

981
00:36:39,540 --> 00:36:45,080
He's writing this pornography, but he's also writing very vicious political polemics.

982
00:36:45,480 --> 00:36:47,920
So, he accuses his own estate, the nobility.

983
00:36:48,040 --> 00:36:54,100
He says, 30,000 calculating vampires who have sucked the blood of 20 million Frenchmen.

984
00:36:54,880 --> 00:37:01,480
And it will amaze you to know that having written that, he doesn't manage to win election from the rest of the nobility.

985
00:37:01,880 --> 00:37:04,740
But the good news is that he can run for the third estate, which he does.

986
00:37:04,740 --> 00:37:06,160
And he's elected from Aix-en-Provence.

987
00:37:06,540 --> 00:37:15,280
The thing is that despite his appearance, he's basically a huge man when he's got this terrible skin and he's shouting about vampires and stuff.

988
00:37:15,580 --> 00:37:19,200
But actually, behind all that, he's quite moderate.

989
00:37:19,500 --> 00:37:20,620
So, what he wants…

990
00:37:20,620 --> 00:37:22,360
He wants a kind of British constitution.

991
00:37:22,860 --> 00:37:24,400
He totally wants the British constitution.

992
00:37:25,080 --> 00:37:26,900
That's very sensible and reflects well on him.

993
00:37:26,960 --> 00:37:32,800
But he's determined to be seen as the supreme champion of the people in this whole imbroglio.

994
00:37:32,800 --> 00:37:40,800
So, when they have that procession, he apparently walks in a peculiar way, throwing his head back so that everyone can see this huge head.

995
00:37:41,120 --> 00:37:44,340
And sort of bobbing his head around in a slightly disturbing manner.

996
00:37:44,720 --> 00:37:45,520
So, he's like Harry Maguire.

997
00:37:45,720 --> 00:37:47,400
Yes, he's got Harry Maguire's head.

998
00:37:47,820 --> 00:37:48,260
Exactly.

999
00:37:48,460 --> 00:37:49,820
And if you don't know who Harry Maguire is…

1000
00:37:49,820 --> 00:37:51,100
Harry Maguire crossed with a volcano.

1001
00:37:51,500 --> 00:37:51,640
Yeah.

1002
00:37:51,720 --> 00:37:53,660
So, people are interested in the football metaphor.

1003
00:37:53,820 --> 00:37:59,320
My brother once described Martin Keown, the football pundit, as looking like a fish trapped in a meteor.

1004
00:38:00,300 --> 00:38:02,780
And that's Mirabeau's favourite.

1005
00:38:02,780 --> 00:38:06,060
Right, a couple of other good characters from the Third Estate.

1006
00:38:06,200 --> 00:38:08,460
You've got Siez, the abbé who wrote the…

1007
00:38:09,040 --> 00:38:10,080
What's the Third Estate?

1008
00:38:10,380 --> 00:38:10,460
Yeah.

1009
00:38:10,640 --> 00:38:14,620
There's our friend from Grenoble, Mr. Meunier, the first revolutionary.

1010
00:38:16,000 --> 00:38:19,660
And there's an obscure young lawyer from Arras.

1011
00:38:19,660 --> 00:38:20,440
Yeah, the young judge.

1012
00:38:20,520 --> 00:38:22,600
He's called Maximilien Robespierre.

1013
00:38:22,800 --> 00:38:25,810
So, he is the counterpoint to Mirabeau.

1014
00:38:26,540 --> 00:38:26,700
Yeah.

1015
00:38:26,700 --> 00:38:27,520
Thin, pale.

1016
00:38:27,820 --> 00:38:28,420
He's me.

1017
00:38:29,140 --> 00:38:29,920
Mirabeau is you.

1018
00:38:31,400 --> 00:38:32,340
Thanks, Tom.

1019
00:38:32,440 --> 00:38:32,760
Yeah.

1020
00:38:32,780 --> 00:38:34,120
I'd take Mirabeau.

1021
00:38:34,180 --> 00:38:35,140
I'd take him over.

1022
00:38:35,440 --> 00:38:36,780
Yeah, you'd rather be Mirabeau than Robespierre.

1023
00:38:36,780 --> 00:38:37,180
I definitely would.

1024
00:38:37,260 --> 00:38:40,520
I mean, it's terrible that my physical form means that I have to be Robespierre.

1025
00:38:40,520 --> 00:38:40,600
Yeah.

1026
00:38:40,600 --> 00:38:45,180
But Robespierre is more typical of the people in the Third Estate because they're basically all lawyers.

1027
00:38:45,840 --> 00:38:51,060
Of 600 of them, 180 are lawyers and 220 are judges or magistrates.

1028
00:38:51,900 --> 00:38:57,460
So, in other words, these Third Estate people, the commons so-called, they're not the common people of France.

1029
00:38:57,700 --> 00:39:02,760
They are upwardly mobile winners, people who actually are not very representative of the mass of the French people.

1030
00:39:02,760 --> 00:39:08,160
But people who are incredibly articulate and ambitious and suffused.

1031
00:39:08,380 --> 00:39:09,040
And resentful.

1032
00:39:09,080 --> 00:39:09,820
And resentful, exactly.

1033
00:39:10,080 --> 00:39:11,240
And resentful of those above them.

1034
00:39:11,380 --> 00:39:11,540
Yeah.

1035
00:39:11,600 --> 00:39:18,360
And the trouble is that those above them don't make any effort to hide that they're above them or to include them.

1036
00:39:18,580 --> 00:39:20,400
Well, isn't that the whole point of the Ancien Régime?

1037
00:39:20,460 --> 00:39:21,060
It's all about hiring.

1038
00:39:21,060 --> 00:39:21,420
Of course it is.

1039
00:39:21,560 --> 00:39:22,580
So, the very first…

1040
00:39:22,580 --> 00:39:24,400
So, that was 4th of May, the procession.

1041
00:39:24,400 --> 00:39:32,240
And the 5th of May, they meet in the Salle des Menus Plaisir, the hall of the lesser pleasures of Versailles.

1042
00:39:33,000 --> 00:39:34,580
And Louis is on his throne.

1043
00:39:34,740 --> 00:39:36,420
He's got Marie Antoinette at his side.

1044
00:39:36,760 --> 00:39:38,240
There's a table for his ministers.

1045
00:39:38,520 --> 00:39:41,140
Then you have the benches of the church and the nobility.

1046
00:39:41,500 --> 00:39:45,400
And then the Third Estate are all shoved down to the far end of the hall.

1047
00:39:46,300 --> 00:39:47,760
And the king gives a speech.

1048
00:39:47,860 --> 00:39:50,500
And he says, ah, brilliant.

1049
00:39:50,640 --> 00:39:51,220
You're all here.

1050
00:39:51,340 --> 00:39:52,640
I've been looking forward to this.

1051
00:39:53,020 --> 00:39:53,420
Everyone's…

1052
00:39:53,420 --> 00:39:54,420
Should have done it before.

1053
00:39:54,560 --> 00:39:54,760
Great.

1054
00:39:54,840 --> 00:39:56,100
He does say…

1055
00:39:56,100 --> 00:39:58,280
There's been a lot of talk of innovations.

1056
00:39:58,840 --> 00:40:00,660
I think this is much exaggerated.

1057
00:40:00,960 --> 00:40:02,220
I don't want to hear any more of that.

1058
00:40:03,340 --> 00:40:05,580
Then there's a strange business with his hat.

1059
00:40:06,400 --> 00:40:08,880
So, he doffs his hat at the end of the speech.

1060
00:40:09,040 --> 00:40:11,260
And then the nobility doff their hats.

1061
00:40:11,500 --> 00:40:12,120
Their beaver hats.

1062
00:40:12,340 --> 00:40:13,100
Their beaver hats.

1063
00:40:13,240 --> 00:40:13,520
Exactly.

1064
00:40:13,920 --> 00:40:15,860
Now, the Third Estate are confused at this.

1065
00:40:15,920 --> 00:40:19,060
Some of them take their hats off or put their hats on or whatever.

1066
00:40:19,240 --> 00:40:20,600
Their hats are going on and off.

1067
00:40:20,760 --> 00:40:21,060
Yeah.

1068
00:40:21,200 --> 00:40:22,680
And this is against all protocol.

1069
00:40:22,820 --> 00:40:24,200
They shouldn't be doing anything with their hats.

1070
00:40:24,340 --> 00:40:25,420
Louis is really confused.

1071
00:40:26,100 --> 00:40:28,060
He has to take his hat back off again.

1072
00:40:28,100 --> 00:40:30,520
They all start putting their hats off and then on.

1073
00:40:30,640 --> 00:40:32,040
But, Dominic, it's fine.

1074
00:40:32,240 --> 00:40:32,560
Isn't it?

1075
00:40:32,660 --> 00:40:39,100
Because despite all the confusion, there's then the huge excitement of Jacques Necker

1076
00:40:39,100 --> 00:40:40,580
giving a speech…

1077
00:40:40,580 --> 00:40:40,780
Oh, yeah.

1078
00:40:40,920 --> 00:40:43,400
…on fiscal policy and finance.

1079
00:40:44,000 --> 00:40:45,440
So, that must cheer them all up.

1080
00:40:45,560 --> 00:40:46,840
Well, everyone's really looking forward to this.

1081
00:40:47,080 --> 00:40:50,680
So, this is the moment when the great man, the Swiss banker…

1082
00:40:50,680 --> 00:40:50,800
Yeah.

1083
00:40:50,960 --> 00:40:52,140
…is going to put everything to rights.

1084
00:40:52,240 --> 00:40:54,640
And he stands up and he reads the first half hour of his speech.

1085
00:40:54,720 --> 00:40:58,000
And then he says, listen, there's two and a half hours more of this to go.

1086
00:40:58,300 --> 00:41:00,180
I can't be bothered to read it myself.

1087
00:41:00,320 --> 00:41:02,220
And he gives it to the Secretary of the Agricultural…

1088
00:41:02,220 --> 00:41:04,420
…Agriculture Committee and says, you read it.

1089
00:41:04,500 --> 00:41:07,200
And the guy from the Agriculture Committee has been chosen just because he has a very

1090
00:41:07,200 --> 00:41:08,560
loud and strident voice.

1091
00:41:09,520 --> 00:41:15,260
And he reads, and Necker has basically written an incredibly boring speech about the deficit

1092
00:41:15,260 --> 00:41:16,120
and about finance.

1093
00:41:16,480 --> 00:41:18,120
Because the King starts yawning, doesn't he?

1094
00:41:18,140 --> 00:41:19,180
And everyone starts falling asleep.

1095
00:41:19,460 --> 00:41:20,160
People fall asleep.

1096
00:41:20,300 --> 00:41:22,720
Everybody says afterwards, oh, my God, it's so disappointing.

1097
00:41:23,160 --> 00:41:23,860
Necker is…

1098
00:41:23,860 --> 00:41:25,300
What a useless person Necker is.

1099
00:41:25,380 --> 00:41:26,380
I've totally changed my mind.

1100
00:41:26,540 --> 00:41:29,500
So, a bit like the British general election, the recent British general election.

1101
00:41:30,380 --> 00:41:31,940
Very significant, but boring.

1102
00:41:31,940 --> 00:41:36,580
So, the next day, the 6th of May, they all have to arrive and they're going to begin

1103
00:41:36,580 --> 00:41:37,320
their deliberations.

1104
00:41:37,460 --> 00:41:41,440
And first of all, the various estates have to be registered, have to verify their credentials.

1105
00:41:41,860 --> 00:41:45,860
And when they all arrive, the King's men say, right, you're going to go to your separate

1106
00:41:45,860 --> 00:41:49,980
chambers, nobility over here, clergy over here, third estate this way.

1107
00:41:50,120 --> 00:41:51,760
And Mirabeau says, stop.

1108
00:41:53,060 --> 00:41:55,340
We cannot go to a separate chamber.

1109
00:41:55,880 --> 00:42:01,160
Because once we agree to that principle, the idea of all sitting together and us dominating

1110
00:42:01,160 --> 00:42:02,140
is lost.

1111
00:42:02,880 --> 00:42:07,260
We must verify our credentials with everybody else.

1112
00:42:07,440 --> 00:42:09,720
And he is really passionate about it.

1113
00:42:09,800 --> 00:42:13,560
And one of the deputies says he's like a ferocious beast, an oranger.

1114
00:42:13,900 --> 00:42:15,520
He speaks in convulsions.

1115
00:42:15,520 --> 00:42:16,800
His face is contorted.

1116
00:42:17,020 --> 00:42:19,260
He spits out his words in a fury.

1117
00:42:19,620 --> 00:42:22,100
And that, obviously, is the French Revolutionary style.

1118
00:42:22,380 --> 00:42:23,280
Everything is overwrought.

1119
00:42:23,760 --> 00:42:24,660
Everything is…

1120
00:42:24,660 --> 00:42:26,380
You wear it, kind of.

1121
00:42:26,380 --> 00:42:27,720
You wear your sentiments physically.

1122
00:42:28,020 --> 00:42:31,080
And you turn your rhetoric up to the absolute.

1123
00:42:31,080 --> 00:42:31,840
Absolute maximum.

1124
00:42:32,040 --> 00:42:36,500
And yet there is also a sense, isn't there, that you're trying to emulate a Roman style,

1125
00:42:36,760 --> 00:42:38,940
a kind of Ciceronian style.

1126
00:42:38,940 --> 00:42:39,940
Yeah, definitely there is.

1127
00:42:40,220 --> 00:42:41,560
Formal and faintly chilly.

1128
00:42:41,820 --> 00:42:43,260
Well, I think there's a couple of things, actually.

1129
00:42:43,460 --> 00:42:47,740
So part of it is definitely these people have studied the classical period at school, haven't

1130
00:42:47,740 --> 00:42:48,460
they, these young lawyers?

1131
00:42:48,580 --> 00:42:49,020
Robespierre.

1132
00:42:49,140 --> 00:42:49,920
Very saturated.

1133
00:42:50,040 --> 00:42:51,260
Robespierre, a classic example.

1134
00:42:51,360 --> 00:42:53,780
They've read all the speeches, particularly Cicero.

1135
00:42:54,020 --> 00:42:56,740
And they're obsessed with Cicero and copying Cicero's rhetoric.

1136
00:42:57,060 --> 00:42:58,960
So they see themselves as the new Romans.

1137
00:42:59,540 --> 00:43:01,000
But also there is an argument.

1138
00:43:02,880 --> 00:43:07,440
It's been a great age the last 10, 20 years in France of the public theatre, popular

1139
00:43:07,440 --> 00:43:07,920
theatre.

1140
00:43:08,180 --> 00:43:13,720
Well, we talked about how massively important the theatre is in a couple of episodes ago.

1141
00:43:13,900 --> 00:43:14,000
Yeah, we did.

1142
00:43:14,120 --> 00:43:14,680
We did.

1143
00:43:15,340 --> 00:43:16,820
With The Marriage of a Figaro.

1144
00:43:17,000 --> 00:43:19,960
Yeah, The Marriage of a Figaro is a perfect example because The Marriage of a Figaro is

1145
00:43:19,960 --> 00:43:26,100
very emotional, tear-stained, over-the-top, fun, this sense of spectacle.

1146
00:43:26,760 --> 00:43:30,980
And for the first time, really, politics is deliberately emulated.

1147
00:43:31,000 --> 00:43:35,080
So there is a public gallery for the deliberations.

1148
00:43:35,580 --> 00:43:43,140
The Third Estate are basically having a massive stalemate with the court and refusing to go

1149
00:43:43,140 --> 00:43:46,780
ahead and be verified along with the other chambers.

1150
00:43:47,020 --> 00:43:48,300
And this stalemate continues.

1151
00:43:48,780 --> 00:43:55,400
And every day, thousands of people will get coaches from Paris, come out to Versailles

1152
00:43:55,400 --> 00:43:56,380
to watch the show.

1153
00:43:56,680 --> 00:44:00,640
The most famous one is an English traveler called Arthur Young, who is one of the great

1154
00:44:00,640 --> 00:44:02,960
go-to sources on the early French Revolution.

1155
00:44:03,220 --> 00:44:04,660
He's quoted in every single book.

1156
00:44:05,080 --> 00:44:06,680
And he gets a coach out there.

1157
00:44:06,780 --> 00:44:08,280
He has all the big names speaking.

1158
00:44:08,960 --> 00:44:10,840
He's excited about all the oratory.

1159
00:44:11,240 --> 00:44:17,200
And he says, what's shocking for him, coming from Britain, is that the spectators in the

1160
00:44:17,200 --> 00:44:22,020
galleries clap and cheer and boo as though it is a play.

1161
00:44:22,200 --> 00:44:23,580
And he says, this is very bad.

1162
00:44:23,700 --> 00:44:26,880
This is reducing politics to mere entertainment.

1163
00:44:27,860 --> 00:44:30,620
But actually, this becomes central to the show.

1164
00:44:30,640 --> 00:44:31,020
The revolution.

1165
00:44:33,060 --> 00:44:33,940
Emotional outpourings.

1166
00:44:34,720 --> 00:44:43,560
And this idea of the orator, who is copying Roman models like Cicero, commanding people

1167
00:44:43,560 --> 00:44:44,620
by the strength of his voice.

1168
00:44:44,700 --> 00:44:46,000
So Mirabeau had a really good voice.

1169
00:44:48,000 --> 00:44:51,700
Perhaps bursting into tears while he's talking, which people think is brilliant.

1170
00:44:52,040 --> 00:44:56,140
But also, presumably, it's because unlike in Britain, where you have the House of Commons,

1171
00:44:56,200 --> 00:44:59,200
you have the House of Lords, and you have elections where people go on the hustings.

1172
00:44:59,200 --> 00:45:04,140
And so the traditions of political engagement are organic.

1173
00:45:04,280 --> 00:45:05,040
They've evolved.

1174
00:45:05,560 --> 00:45:10,920
They are not suddenly being portrayed to people as something new.

1175
00:45:11,140 --> 00:45:14,820
But in this occasion, no one's seen anything like this.

1176
00:45:14,960 --> 00:45:15,660
Yeah, of course.

1177
00:45:15,940 --> 00:45:17,840
They don't have regular elections.

1178
00:45:18,040 --> 00:45:22,620
They don't have the throwing of dead cats and the attacks with halberds that are such

1179
00:45:22,620 --> 00:45:24,220
an important part of the British Constitution.

1180
00:45:24,300 --> 00:45:28,080
And so there must be a feeling that they're kind of...

1181
00:45:28,080 --> 00:45:33,500
You know, Mirabeau and everybody else is kind of feeling their way into roles that

1182
00:45:33,500 --> 00:45:34,960
have not yet been defined.

1183
00:45:34,980 --> 00:45:35,720
Yeah, making it up.

1184
00:45:35,760 --> 00:45:36,380
They're making it...

1185
00:45:36,380 --> 00:45:37,040
What is politics?

1186
00:45:37,400 --> 00:45:38,200
And what is the style of politics?

1187
00:45:38,200 --> 00:45:39,100
What is the style of politics?

1188
00:45:39,140 --> 00:45:39,480
Exactly.

1189
00:45:39,520 --> 00:45:44,620
And they're drawing their style of politics from the theatre, from literature, from the

1190
00:45:44,620 --> 00:45:46,080
kind of cultural mood of the time.

1191
00:45:46,220 --> 00:45:53,100
And so this is clearly very much true of the members of the Third Estate, that they are

1192
00:45:53,100 --> 00:45:57,580
playing a role simply by not moving, by sticking to their guns.

1193
00:45:58,340 --> 00:46:02,040
What about the First and Second Estates?

1194
00:46:02,040 --> 00:46:03,740
Well, they've gone off to their own...

1195
00:46:03,740 --> 00:46:08,680
But they must be watching this performance as well and thinking, oh, maybe we could...

1196
00:46:08,680 --> 00:46:09,900
Maybe we should join it.

1197
00:46:09,960 --> 00:46:10,100
Yeah.

1198
00:46:10,360 --> 00:46:10,800
Eventually.

1199
00:46:10,860 --> 00:46:12,140
They don't join straight away, though.

1200
00:46:12,240 --> 00:46:14,020
So there's quite a long...

1201
00:46:14,020 --> 00:46:19,740
There's about a month before the first dribs and drabs from them come to join the Third

1202
00:46:19,740 --> 00:46:19,940
Estate.

1203
00:46:20,060 --> 00:46:21,640
So the stalemate is quite long lasting.

1204
00:46:21,740 --> 00:46:25,680
And in all that time, the stalemate is being reported back in Paris.

1205
00:46:26,060 --> 00:46:27,660
So Mirabeau, for example...

1206
00:46:27,660 --> 00:46:36,060
His mistress owns a print shop, which is very convenient for him because he writes letters

1207
00:46:36,060 --> 00:46:38,100
to his constituents telling them what's going on.

1208
00:46:38,600 --> 00:46:43,900
And they go to the printers and they're released in Paris before they even get back to Aix-en-Provence.

1209
00:46:44,280 --> 00:46:46,200
So there's this stream of stuff.

1210
00:46:46,680 --> 00:46:48,860
So actually, it's not just a theatrical event.

1211
00:46:49,400 --> 00:46:50,840
It's also a media event.

1212
00:46:51,120 --> 00:46:51,740
Yeah, of course.

1213
00:46:51,880 --> 00:46:57,060
The first sort of mediated, mediatized...

1214
00:46:57,060 --> 00:46:58,840
political event of its kind.

1215
00:46:59,040 --> 00:47:02,420
Because there's this whole thing, isn't there, that Robert Darnton mentions about how people

1216
00:47:02,420 --> 00:47:07,420
wear buttons with images of top political actors and things.

1217
00:47:07,660 --> 00:47:15,220
So there is a sense in which the relationship of the people to the theatre can be mapped

1218
00:47:15,220 --> 00:47:18,800
onto their relationship to what is going on in Versailles.

1219
00:47:18,840 --> 00:47:20,140
Yeah, I think that's definitely true.

1220
00:47:20,460 --> 00:47:24,500
I think there is a sense that it's an entertainment.

1221
00:47:24,700 --> 00:47:25,360
It's a spectacle.

1222
00:47:25,640 --> 00:47:26,360
It's a sport.

1223
00:47:26,860 --> 00:47:29,300
It's a fashion.

1224
00:47:29,920 --> 00:47:31,880
You know, all these things are kind of...

1225
00:47:31,880 --> 00:47:37,320
And of course, it's of pressing importance because of the catastrophic state that people

1226
00:47:37,320 --> 00:47:39,780
are in across the country and in Paris as well.

1227
00:47:39,840 --> 00:47:39,980
Absolutely.

1228
00:47:40,080 --> 00:47:44,920
So by the end of May, early June, there are really, really bleak apocalyptic reports coming

1229
00:47:44,920 --> 00:47:45,580
in from the countryside.

1230
00:47:46,120 --> 00:47:51,600
There are posters going up in Paris saying that if something changes, there are posters

1231
00:47:51,600 --> 00:47:54,480
going up in Paris saying that if nothing changes, there's going to be an insurrection.

1232
00:47:54,800 --> 00:47:55,500
There's, you know...

1233
00:47:55,500 --> 00:47:59,260
You can imagine the scene, calling for an uprising, those kind of posters.

1234
00:47:59,840 --> 00:48:02,200
And that lends an urgency to all this.

1235
00:48:02,300 --> 00:48:07,360
So on the 10th of June, the Abbé Sieyès says, look, we cannot wait any longer.

1236
00:48:08,220 --> 00:48:12,180
You know, the courts seem determined to not let us sit with the other states.

1237
00:48:13,380 --> 00:48:14,120
Here's what we do.

1238
00:48:14,780 --> 00:48:16,360
We will give them an ultimatum.

1239
00:48:17,140 --> 00:48:18,460
You come and sit with us.

1240
00:48:19,560 --> 00:48:24,500
And whether they do or not, we will declare ourselves the National Assembly.

1241
00:48:24,500 --> 00:48:27,640
We will be the sole representatives of the nation.

1242
00:48:27,880 --> 00:48:31,340
And we will take the nation's fate into our hands.

1243
00:48:31,680 --> 00:48:33,700
I mean, this is an unbelievable thing.

1244
00:48:33,920 --> 00:48:34,360
It is.

1245
00:48:34,480 --> 00:48:35,640
They're in Versailles.

1246
00:48:35,860 --> 00:48:36,080
Yeah.

1247
00:48:36,260 --> 00:48:39,940
The monarch is only hundreds of yards away in some other room.

1248
00:48:40,100 --> 00:48:44,380
And they're basically saying, we will take this into our own hands.

1249
00:48:44,740 --> 00:48:48,760
But also they're laying claim to the sovereignty that traditionally has been that of the monarch.

1250
00:48:48,980 --> 00:48:49,340
Exactly.

1251
00:48:49,480 --> 00:48:53,160
So, I mean, at this point, I don't think they recognise that, do they?

1252
00:48:53,300 --> 00:48:54,420
Well, I think they still think...

1253
00:48:54,420 --> 00:48:58,160
This is kind of going to be a rerun of Britain's glorious revolution.

1254
00:48:58,980 --> 00:49:00,220
And it won't go too far.

1255
00:49:00,320 --> 00:49:02,420
Eventually, Louis will give in and it'll be all fine.

1256
00:49:02,540 --> 00:49:04,000
I think a lot of them still do think that.

1257
00:49:04,560 --> 00:49:09,120
And you asked, Tom, and I didn't answer it properly, for which I apologise to you, hand on heart,

1258
00:49:09,880 --> 00:49:11,380
about the first two estates.

1259
00:49:11,440 --> 00:49:13,580
Some of them are now beginning to come across.

1260
00:49:13,740 --> 00:49:15,520
On the 13th of June, there was a real breakthrough.

1261
00:49:15,820 --> 00:49:17,540
Some parish priests come across.

1262
00:49:17,760 --> 00:49:20,500
And actually, you asked about the two estates.

1263
00:49:20,540 --> 00:49:22,520
The first estate, the clergy,

1264
00:49:22,520 --> 00:49:27,660
they are actually the ones who are closest to the mass of ordinary French men and women.

1265
00:49:27,780 --> 00:49:34,600
Because most of the clergy's deputies are not archbishops with kind of sumptuous Cardinal Wolsey-style robes.

1266
00:49:34,920 --> 00:49:37,580
They're ordinary parish priests who are probably...

1267
00:49:37,580 --> 00:49:38,820
Who are often very poor themselves.

1268
00:49:38,960 --> 00:49:44,380
Very poor and who actually understand the peasants better than some of the third estate lawyers do.

1269
00:49:44,380 --> 00:49:45,360
The lawyers and so on.

1270
00:49:45,480 --> 00:49:50,260
So more and more of these priests come over in the next few days, rising to about 100 of the priests.

1271
00:49:51,680 --> 00:49:55,280
And on the 17th of June, they say, enough!

1272
00:49:55,320 --> 00:49:55,940
Time has run out.

1273
00:49:56,060 --> 00:49:58,480
We are going to now call ourselves some...

1274
00:49:58,480 --> 00:50:01,060
You know, we're going to call ourselves, as it were, a National Assembly.

1275
00:50:01,800 --> 00:50:05,340
The amusing thing is they can't decide what the name should be.

1276
00:50:05,760 --> 00:50:15,060
So Siez says, our title, which will resound down the generations, is the Known and Verifiable Representatives.

1277
00:50:15,980 --> 00:50:19,220
So this is very like us trying to decide what to call episodes of our show.

1278
00:50:20,480 --> 00:50:25,040
It's actually like you wanting the podcast to be called Podpast, which...

1279
00:50:25,680 --> 00:50:27,620
I still regret that.

1280
00:50:27,620 --> 00:50:27,780
Yeah.

1281
00:50:28,560 --> 00:50:29,660
You're about to stick with that.

1282
00:50:29,780 --> 00:50:32,220
And this is the first time I've ever heard you say that you regret it.

1283
00:50:32,380 --> 00:50:34,600
I just think it would be a very good name, but whatever.

1284
00:50:34,820 --> 00:50:35,260
Oh, sorry.

1285
00:50:35,360 --> 00:50:37,000
You don't regret having come up with it?

1286
00:50:37,280 --> 00:50:38,100
No, not at all.

1287
00:50:38,240 --> 00:50:39,120
No, I'm very proud of it.

1288
00:50:39,560 --> 00:50:45,280
Well, listen, I mean, when the rest of history is dead, you can do Podpast, but I'm not doing Podpast.

1289
00:50:45,920 --> 00:50:49,700
So Amunier, the guy from Grenoble, he says that's a terrible name, the Known and Verifiable Representatives.

1290
00:50:50,040 --> 00:50:50,240
He says...

1291
00:50:50,260 --> 00:50:51,240
I've got a very catchy name.

1292
00:50:51,380 --> 00:50:53,560
This is Tom Holland of the Third Estate.

1293
00:50:53,900 --> 00:50:54,980
He's got a brilliant name.

1294
00:50:55,180 --> 00:51:01,520
I think we should be called the Legitimate Assembly of the Representatives of the Larger Part of the Nation Acting in the Absence of the Smaller Part.

1295
00:51:01,760 --> 00:51:03,260
No, I'm about compression, Podpast.

1296
00:51:03,540 --> 00:51:03,740
Anyway.

1297
00:51:03,860 --> 00:51:05,000
Anyway, listen, let's not get into that.

1298
00:51:05,060 --> 00:51:05,540
Anyway, yeah.

1299
00:51:05,620 --> 00:51:09,580
So they have a succession of terrible names, but eventually they decide, let's go for National Assembly.

1300
00:51:10,020 --> 00:51:10,880
Let's cut to the chase.

1301
00:51:11,080 --> 00:51:15,820
Now, there's a sense, I think, at this point of them self-radicalising anything being possible.

1302
00:51:16,500 --> 00:51:19,700
They say that basically all existing taxation is illegal.

1303
00:51:19,700 --> 00:51:22,180
Because they haven't authorised it.

1304
00:51:22,500 --> 00:51:29,060
But for the time being, they're happy to let the existing taxes continue until they have drawn up the new...

1305
00:51:29,060 --> 00:51:33,760
Remember, they, not the king, have drawn up the new tax system.

1306
00:51:35,200 --> 00:51:38,060
So this is, as you said, I mean, they're seizing sovereignty.

1307
00:51:38,260 --> 00:51:39,460
It is really an incredible thing.

1308
00:51:40,660 --> 00:51:44,740
Louis is very, very deeply depressed during all this.

1309
00:51:44,960 --> 00:51:49,180
I mean, he's got, as you said, Tom, before, he's got quite large...

1310
00:51:49,180 --> 00:51:50,420
He's eating a lot.

1311
00:51:50,560 --> 00:51:51,980
He's messing around with his locks.

1312
00:51:52,460 --> 00:51:55,380
He feels very sad that this is spiralling out of control.

1313
00:51:55,580 --> 00:51:57,140
And then the terrible thing happens, doesn't it?

1314
00:51:57,140 --> 00:51:57,600
Yeah, awful.

1315
00:51:57,700 --> 00:52:01,500
Because the Dauphin, his son, his much-loved son, dies.

1316
00:52:01,600 --> 00:52:02,540
On the 4th of June.

1317
00:52:02,660 --> 00:52:03,440
Dies of TB.

1318
00:52:03,640 --> 00:52:04,800
And actually dies...

1319
00:52:04,800 --> 00:52:08,200
We won't go into all the details because I think actually it's unnecessary and horrible.

1320
00:52:08,620 --> 00:52:14,140
But this little boy dies a very agonising, lingering death from TB.

1321
00:52:14,380 --> 00:52:15,380
He's just seven years old.

1322
00:52:15,460 --> 00:52:19,100
And Louis and Marie Antoinette are understanding.

1323
00:52:19,100 --> 00:52:21,280
Understandably, absolutely distraught.

1324
00:52:21,940 --> 00:52:27,000
And Louis, in a weird way, actually, I think almost all the history books, even the best ones, underplay this.

1325
00:52:27,520 --> 00:52:30,460
They just say, oh, by the way, his son died and he's very upset and he withdraws.

1326
00:52:30,460 --> 00:52:35,100
But I mean, this for him is a loss of unbelievable proportions.

1327
00:52:35,900 --> 00:52:36,620
Just horrendous.

1328
00:52:36,920 --> 00:52:40,440
Because, you know, unusually for a French king, he hasn't had a mistress.

1329
00:52:40,620 --> 00:52:42,280
He's very much a family man.

1330
00:52:42,500 --> 00:52:42,740
Yeah.

1331
00:52:42,940 --> 00:52:45,340
And Marie Antoinette as well is a very good mother.

1332
00:52:45,500 --> 00:52:45,740
Yeah.

1333
00:52:45,740 --> 00:52:49,180
So they are engaged with their children in a way that is unusual.

1334
00:52:49,440 --> 00:52:49,660
Of course.

1335
00:52:49,760 --> 00:52:49,900
Yeah.

1336
00:52:50,020 --> 00:52:53,360
But reflects the kind of sentimental ideas of Rousseau.

1337
00:52:53,620 --> 00:52:53,640
Yeah.

1338
00:52:53,640 --> 00:52:54,000
Absolutely.

1339
00:52:54,520 --> 00:52:57,900
So he withdraws from public business.

1340
00:52:58,100 --> 00:52:58,860
He leaves.

1341
00:52:58,980 --> 00:53:05,240
He physically leaves Versailles for his country estate at Marly-le-Roi.

1342
00:53:05,500 --> 00:53:08,680
And there he, you know, is lost in mourning.

1343
00:53:08,920 --> 00:53:10,980
So he's not taking charge of all this.

1344
00:53:11,040 --> 00:53:12,140
It's spiralling out of control.

1345
00:53:12,740 --> 00:53:15,720
When he finally rouses himself and comes back to France.

1346
00:53:15,720 --> 00:53:16,720
To Versailles.

1347
00:53:17,340 --> 00:53:22,520
He has a big meeting with Necker and with his brothers who are much more hardline than he is.

1348
00:53:22,600 --> 00:53:23,220
And with the queen.

1349
00:53:24,040 --> 00:53:25,900
What on earth are we going to do?

1350
00:53:26,020 --> 00:53:29,920
And Necker says, I think we should probably give in to the third estate at this point.

1351
00:53:30,040 --> 00:53:30,260
We should.

1352
00:53:30,280 --> 00:53:31,260
We've got to meet them halfway.

1353
00:53:32,360 --> 00:53:37,560
And Marie Antoinette and his brothers say this business has gone massively out of control.

1354
00:53:38,040 --> 00:53:39,100
It's Necker's fault.

1355
00:53:39,240 --> 00:53:40,880
We should shut all this down now.

1356
00:53:41,500 --> 00:53:42,740
And Louis can't decide.

1357
00:53:42,980 --> 00:53:43,480
So he decides.

1358
00:53:43,620 --> 00:53:45,520
So what he does is a tip.

1359
00:53:45,520 --> 00:53:48,620
A typical Louis thing of a bit of change, but not too much.

1360
00:53:49,300 --> 00:53:50,600
Which doesn't satisfy anybody.

1361
00:53:50,840 --> 00:53:54,240
He says, I'll bring all the estates together in what he calls a séance real.

1362
00:53:54,420 --> 00:53:55,500
A grand meeting.

1363
00:53:56,280 --> 00:53:58,180
And I'll offer them some reforms.

1364
00:53:58,800 --> 00:54:03,300
But they have to abandon all this rubbish and tosh about a national assembly.

1365
00:54:03,580 --> 00:54:04,360
Like this is no good.

1366
00:54:06,060 --> 00:54:08,060
So I want to have this big meeting.

1367
00:54:08,760 --> 00:54:10,200
And they're going to have it in the hall.

1368
00:54:10,460 --> 00:54:13,060
The hall where the third estate had been meeting.

1369
00:54:13,300 --> 00:54:14,800
And they have to set it up.

1370
00:54:15,520 --> 00:54:17,120
They literally have to set up the room.

1371
00:54:17,680 --> 00:54:24,620
On the morning of the 20th of June, his workmen go in to build the platforms necessary for this meeting.

1372
00:54:24,740 --> 00:54:26,340
Where he is going to lay down the law.

1373
00:54:27,680 --> 00:54:32,380
Now, meanwhile, they put up signs outside saying, you know, the third estate can't meet here today.

1374
00:54:32,440 --> 00:54:33,840
We're setting up the room for this big meeting.

1375
00:54:33,980 --> 00:54:35,260
And he puts guards outside.

1376
00:54:36,020 --> 00:54:40,320
The third estate all arrive to find the doors are closed.

1377
00:54:40,540 --> 00:54:42,020
And there are guards blocking the way.

1378
00:54:42,120 --> 00:54:44,780
And there are signs coming up about a big meeting with the king.

1379
00:54:44,780 --> 00:54:48,880
And there are hundreds of armed guards standing around blocking their way.

1380
00:54:49,580 --> 00:54:51,360
And it is also pouring with rain.

1381
00:54:52,120 --> 00:54:53,620
So they're all getting soaked.

1382
00:54:53,720 --> 00:54:55,700
And they're expected to go into the hall and have their meeting.

1383
00:54:56,220 --> 00:54:57,360
What are they going to do?

1384
00:54:57,880 --> 00:55:00,640
And lots of them say, well, I'm not just going to go home to bed.

1385
00:55:00,880 --> 00:55:01,980
I mean, we should have a meeting.

1386
00:55:02,120 --> 00:55:09,860
We should have our meeting on our own as the national assembly as normal.

1387
00:55:10,540 --> 00:55:14,220
And one of them, a man called Dr. Geek.

1388
00:55:15,380 --> 00:55:17,100
Tom, you love this.

1389
00:55:17,440 --> 00:55:28,640
Well, I mean, I love the way that much-loved characters and, indeed, implements of execution are kind of subtly introduced in unexpected angled ways.

1390
00:55:28,960 --> 00:55:30,580
So what's the name of this doctor?

1391
00:55:30,860 --> 00:55:37,440
Dr. Guillotin, who is mindful of interesting devices, Tom.

1392
00:55:38,600 --> 00:55:40,920
Rational devices for law and order.

1393
00:55:41,200 --> 00:55:44,200
Dr. Guillotin says, oh, a friend of mine.

1394
00:55:45,320 --> 00:55:47,500
Owns a tennis court around the corner.

1395
00:55:47,760 --> 00:55:48,960
We could go and meet there.

1396
00:55:49,460 --> 00:55:52,900
It's in the rue du Vieux Versailles, the old Versailles Street.

1397
00:55:53,200 --> 00:56:00,180
Now, often you read popular accounts and they say the tennis court was the royal tennis court at Versailles.

1398
00:56:00,480 --> 00:56:01,640
That's not right at all.

1399
00:56:01,960 --> 00:56:05,460
The game that is being played is a game called real tennis.

1400
00:56:06,240 --> 00:56:07,960
Jeu de paume in French.

1401
00:56:08,280 --> 00:56:10,780
But it's not the king's court or anything like it.

1402
00:56:10,800 --> 00:56:12,640
It's some friend of Dr. Guillotin's.

1403
00:56:13,620 --> 00:56:15,040
Who owns the court.

1404
00:56:15,360 --> 00:56:17,360
Anyway, they all pile into this tennis court.

1405
00:56:17,540 --> 00:56:21,160
So a real tennis is a little bit like squash but with a very hard ball.

1406
00:56:21,960 --> 00:56:23,200
So it's indoors.

1407
00:56:23,420 --> 00:56:27,180
It's kind of an echoey, very stark, like a squash court.

1408
00:56:27,660 --> 00:56:29,260
They're all soaked with the rain.

1409
00:56:29,500 --> 00:56:30,740
They all pile into this place.

1410
00:56:30,840 --> 00:56:35,260
It's very sort of, it's very plain, very drab, very stark.

1411
00:56:35,940 --> 00:56:37,700
But actually that kind of suits them.

1412
00:56:37,840 --> 00:56:38,600
It suits them.

1413
00:56:38,720 --> 00:56:40,700
It does because it's classical, isn't it, potentially?

1414
00:56:41,020 --> 00:56:41,400
It is.

1415
00:56:41,400 --> 00:56:45,860
And it gives them a perfect opportunity to swear an oath.

1416
00:56:45,960 --> 00:56:50,540
Yeah, because Meunier, the man from Grenoble, he says, what we should all do is we should now swear an oath.

1417
00:56:50,660 --> 00:57:05,140
Now, they've all used to this because the big hit painting of the last few years was by David, who you'll be talking about in a subsequent later episode, won't you, one day, when we finally get around to finishing this huge French Revolution epic.

1418
00:57:05,880 --> 00:57:09,420
David had done a painting called The Oath of the Horatii, hadn't he?

1419
00:57:09,620 --> 00:57:11,380
About three brothers who sacrificed their lives.

1420
00:57:11,380 --> 00:57:11,600
Yeah.

1421
00:57:11,600 --> 00:57:16,040
About three brothers who sacrificed themselves for Rome or offered to sacrifice themselves for Rome, sort of martyrs, patriots.

1422
00:57:16,460 --> 00:57:19,000
And they all decide they want to copy this painting.

1423
00:57:19,600 --> 00:57:24,160
So they swear an oath to God and the patrie, the fatherland.

1424
00:57:24,640 --> 00:57:28,760
They will never separate until they've written a new constitution for France.

1425
00:57:28,940 --> 00:57:36,360
And they say, listen, we don't even need a palace or a courtroom because we are the National Assembly and the National Assembly will exist wherever we come together.

1426
00:57:36,680 --> 00:57:39,300
And then they raise their arms, as in David's painting.

1427
00:57:39,300 --> 00:57:48,240
They stretch out their right arms, often not a brilliant look, to be fair, tainted by subsequent misconduct in the 20th century.

1428
00:57:48,320 --> 00:57:49,000
But that's by the by.

1429
00:57:49,100 --> 00:57:50,280
But again, Roman inspiration.

1430
00:57:50,360 --> 00:57:51,800
They stretch out their right arms.

1431
00:57:51,980 --> 00:57:55,260
They put the other hand on their heart and they swear this oath.

1432
00:57:55,440 --> 00:58:07,380
A great act of theatre again, classic act of theatre, inspired by, you know, the media, by paintings, by paintings and the subsequent engravings that reproduce the painting.

1433
00:58:07,380 --> 00:58:10,980
There's incredible kind of meta self-consciousness about the whole thing.

1434
00:58:11,160 --> 00:58:11,960
There is, exactly.

1435
00:58:12,220 --> 00:58:13,780
Everybody is, what do they call it?

1436
00:58:14,100 --> 00:58:14,580
Cosplaying.

1437
00:58:14,780 --> 00:58:15,100
Yeah.

1438
00:58:15,260 --> 00:58:16,540
That's what they're doing, isn't it?

1439
00:58:16,560 --> 00:58:19,000
They're basically dressing up and pretending to be Romans.

1440
00:58:19,240 --> 00:58:20,760
The news spreads very quickly.

1441
00:58:20,860 --> 00:58:22,460
They've sworn this oath in this tennis court.

1442
00:58:22,860 --> 00:58:27,640
There are printed copies of the oath on sale in Paris the next morning.

1443
00:58:29,340 --> 00:58:34,400
Louis' brothers and the other hardliners in the court go absolutely ballistic at this.

1444
00:58:34,500 --> 00:58:35,940
I love their solution to the whole problem.

1445
00:58:35,940 --> 00:58:36,700
It's fantastic, isn't it?

1446
00:58:36,700 --> 00:58:40,480
The comte d'Artois, he just says, brilliant, I'm just going to book the tennis court.

1447
00:58:40,520 --> 00:58:40,720
He does.

1448
00:58:40,800 --> 00:58:42,260
He books the tennis court to stop them.

1449
00:58:42,800 --> 00:58:46,860
And the question that's on my mind that I haven't been able to resolve is, did he play tennis?

1450
00:58:47,440 --> 00:58:48,080
Did he?

1451
00:58:48,100 --> 00:58:51,340
I mean, once you've booked the court, you're mad not to use it, right?

1452
00:58:51,440 --> 00:58:52,200
What else are you going to be doing?

1453
00:58:52,480 --> 00:58:53,480
Especially if it's raining.

1454
00:58:56,040 --> 00:59:04,500
Anyway, the 23rd of June, so three days later, is going to be this big séance real, a big meeting, summit meeting where Louis is going to decide all this.

1455
00:59:05,140 --> 00:59:06,480
And again, crazy.

1456
00:59:06,480 --> 00:59:10,720
I mean, such a failure of statecraft, I suppose.

1457
00:59:12,140 --> 00:59:13,680
He humiliates the third estate.

1458
00:59:14,100 --> 00:59:16,540
The church and the nobility are seated first.

1459
00:59:16,640 --> 00:59:20,020
The third estate have to wait outside in the rain for an hour.

1460
00:59:20,240 --> 00:59:22,360
And then they're brought in through a side door.

1461
00:59:23,340 --> 00:59:26,760
When they get in, they find that Necker is not there.

1462
00:59:26,940 --> 00:59:31,380
It is clear that Necker has lost the argument within the regime.

1463
00:59:32,140 --> 00:59:35,480
And he is refusing to come or has been told to stay away.

1464
00:59:35,480 --> 00:59:37,100
And Louis stands up.

1465
00:59:37,760 --> 00:59:39,680
And Louis speaks for just half an hour.

1466
00:59:40,080 --> 00:59:42,360
He says, I'll give you some of what you want.

1467
00:59:43,180 --> 00:59:48,900
Taxes will now have to be approved by the nation's representatives.

1468
00:59:49,480 --> 00:59:52,160
And I promise that the Estates General will meet regularly.

1469
00:59:52,700 --> 00:59:56,600
But he says, this stuff about the National Assembly is gibberish and nonsense.

1470
00:59:56,800 --> 00:59:57,840
I will not accept it.

1471
00:59:58,140 --> 01:00:01,620
The distinction of the three orders of the state is not up for debate.

1472
01:00:01,900 --> 01:00:04,500
You will have to, you know, the clergy and the nobility.

1473
01:00:05,560 --> 01:00:07,340
They must run their own affairs.

1474
01:00:07,460 --> 01:00:09,380
And they can't be run for them by the third estate.

1475
01:00:09,560 --> 01:00:13,700
Because, of course, if the moderate position is to emulate the British Constitution,

1476
01:00:14,420 --> 01:00:17,280
Britain does have that division, House of Lords and House of Commons.

1477
01:00:17,520 --> 01:00:17,800
Yeah.

1478
01:00:17,920 --> 01:00:21,300
I mean, it's not wholly reactionary in that sense.

1479
01:00:21,500 --> 01:00:23,020
Yeah, it's not wholly reactionary.

1480
01:00:23,040 --> 01:00:26,420
I mean, they're going well beyond the British Constitution now, aren't they?

1481
01:00:26,580 --> 01:00:29,020
And they're going well beyond the American Constitution.

1482
01:00:29,580 --> 01:00:33,480
So they are doing away with the checks and balances that were so important for the United States.

1483
01:00:33,480 --> 01:00:35,680
Because they're all about the general will, aren't they?

1484
01:00:35,860 --> 01:00:39,440
Which is not something that the founding fathers in America were so keen on.

1485
01:00:42,160 --> 01:00:47,780
Now, had Louis said all this two years earlier, maybe the whole story would be different.

1486
01:00:47,860 --> 01:00:48,380
But he didn't.

1487
01:00:48,400 --> 01:00:51,060
And it's too late because, as we said, they've self-radicalized.

1488
01:00:51,660 --> 01:00:58,960
So when he gets up and goes out after half an hour, everybody else starts to file out.

1489
01:00:59,000 --> 01:01:01,020
And the workmen come back in to demolish the platform.

1490
01:01:01,020 --> 01:01:03,960
And the third estate do not move.

1491
01:01:04,780 --> 01:01:05,780
They're sitting there.

1492
01:01:05,880 --> 01:01:06,860
They will not move.

1493
01:01:06,980 --> 01:01:08,840
This is their hall, and they're not going to budge.

1494
01:01:09,300 --> 01:01:13,900
And the Marquis de Trebreze, the guy who is the…

1495
01:01:13,900 --> 01:01:15,440
He's the suit guy.

1496
01:01:15,800 --> 01:01:16,780
Yeah, the suits man.

1497
01:01:17,040 --> 01:01:17,440
Yes.

1498
01:01:17,440 --> 01:01:18,260
The correct thing you wear.

1499
01:01:18,300 --> 01:01:18,600
Derek Guy.

1500
01:01:18,760 --> 01:01:19,900
Yeah, he's him.

1501
01:01:20,040 --> 01:01:20,400
Yeah.

1502
01:01:20,640 --> 01:01:23,660
He says to them, you've got to go.

1503
01:01:23,800 --> 01:01:24,080
Right.

1504
01:01:24,140 --> 01:01:25,060
We're trying to clear the room.

1505
01:01:25,220 --> 01:01:25,700
You've got to go.

1506
01:01:25,780 --> 01:01:29,920
And Mirabeau, by his own account, which is maybe a little bit self-dramatizing, says,

1507
01:01:29,920 --> 01:01:35,820
go and tell those who have sent you that we are here by the will of the people, and

1508
01:01:35,820 --> 01:01:39,040
we will only be driven out at the points of bayonets.

1509
01:01:40,300 --> 01:01:41,720
Very impressive stuff.

1510
01:01:41,760 --> 01:01:47,340
And the Marquis, he retreats because he's such a stickler for protocol.

1511
01:01:47,640 --> 01:01:50,820
He retreats, walking slowly backwards.

1512
01:01:51,260 --> 01:01:53,620
It's a very strange image.

1513
01:01:54,020 --> 01:01:58,300
This man with a face like a crater has given this speech to him.

1514
01:01:58,320 --> 01:01:58,520
No.

1515
01:01:58,520 --> 01:02:00,400
But etiquette is etiquette, isn't it?

1516
01:02:00,460 --> 01:02:00,900
It is.

1517
01:02:01,060 --> 01:02:02,220
And then Louis, poor Louis.

1518
01:02:02,820 --> 01:02:04,360
He's just not a hard man.

1519
01:02:05,100 --> 01:02:05,940
He's not ruthless.

1520
01:02:06,100 --> 01:02:09,980
Because when the Marquis tells Louis this, Louis just says, oh, well, let them stay.

1521
01:02:10,120 --> 01:02:11,340
If they want to stay, let them stay.

1522
01:02:11,420 --> 01:02:11,740
Oh, whatever.

1523
01:02:11,920 --> 01:02:12,340
They do.

1524
01:02:12,500 --> 01:02:14,100
So it's a classic Louis.

1525
01:02:16,040 --> 01:02:19,780
He sort of parades his authority, but then he never, ever enforces it.

1526
01:02:19,800 --> 01:02:20,180
He gives in.

1527
01:02:20,420 --> 01:02:21,020
He always gives in.

1528
01:02:22,360 --> 01:02:25,920
So the next few days, more and more people join this National Assembly from the clergy

1529
01:02:25,920 --> 01:02:26,500
and the nobility.

1530
01:02:26,600 --> 01:02:27,980
And then the 27th of June.

1531
01:02:28,140 --> 01:02:28,500
Yeah.

1532
01:02:28,520 --> 01:02:31,360
Only four days later, Louis does give in.

1533
01:02:31,400 --> 01:02:32,740
His position has eroded.

1534
01:02:32,820 --> 01:02:36,940
It's a classic example of how in revolutionary situations, momentum is all.

1535
01:02:37,140 --> 01:02:38,620
His position has eroded.

1536
01:02:38,660 --> 01:02:40,580
And he says, okay, yeah, I've changed my mind.

1537
01:02:40,580 --> 01:02:40,980
I give up.

1538
01:02:41,400 --> 01:02:45,660
He orders the nobility and the clergy, join the National Assembly now.

1539
01:02:47,040 --> 01:02:49,660
I've, you know, after all this, I've lost.

1540
01:02:51,100 --> 01:02:55,320
There was an explosion of joy in Versailles in a very, very revealing scene.

1541
01:02:55,540 --> 01:02:56,980
So people are chanting.

1542
01:02:57,120 --> 01:02:58,200
The crowds are in Versailles.

1543
01:02:58,200 --> 01:02:58,840
They're shouting.

1544
01:02:58,940 --> 01:02:59,540
Vive Necker!

1545
01:02:59,780 --> 01:03:00,380
Vive le Roi!

1546
01:03:01,600 --> 01:03:08,400
And on the balcony of Louis XIV's bedroom, which overlooks the marble court at Versailles,

1547
01:03:09,040 --> 01:03:13,880
Louis and Marie Antoinette come out onto the balcony and they pretend to look happy and

1548
01:03:13,880 --> 01:03:16,160
to wave to the crowds who are shouting long live the king.

1549
01:03:16,900 --> 01:03:18,980
And actually, I always think it's a telling detail.

1550
